{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527399414,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b0b9b001JQPSpZiPIWbT4W","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22af3a1001kjOUJupIJ8cN4x","type":"tool","id":"call_a7330f57efdd48498d6bda3c","tool":"grep","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"defer|UNKNOWN|compiled|DEFAULT_RESOURCE|INTENT_SIGNALS|SKILLED","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md","limit":80},"output":"Found 53 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md:\n  Line 26: The smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n\n  Line 30: - After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n\n  Line 37: Routing is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n\n  Line 41: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n\n  Line 43: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n\n  Line 49: Intent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n\n  Line 57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n\n  Line 71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n\n  Line 73: Multi-symptom prompts like `debug the broken jsonc descriptor` should load both DEBUGGING and CONFIG. Prompts like `update TypeScript advisor fixture` should load SKILLED plus TYPESCRIPT and CODE_QUALITY.\n\n  Line 77: - **SKILLED** intent scoring favors language standards, hooks, config, scripts, advisor/tests, metadata, and alignment verification.\n\n  Line 78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n\n  Line 86: | ALWAYS | Every invocation | The §8 `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble only: `shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `shared/references/phase-detection.md` and `shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`. The style guide `sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md` loads with CODE_QUALITY, and the shared doctrine loads with its intent: `workflow-debug.md` with DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` with VERIFICATION |\n\n  Line 87: | SURFACE | After SKILLED detection | Surface-specific resources (`sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`) |\n\n  Line 89: | LANGUAGE | SKILLED only | Split JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and Config standards from the matching `sk-code-skilled/references/<lang>/` resources |\n\n  Line 94: ## 4. SKILLED MAP\n\n  Line 96: SKILLED loads from `sk-code-skilled/references/` and `sk-code-skilled/assets/`.\n\n  Line 109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n\n  Line 128: When SKILLED intent is `authoring-new-X`, additionally load the matching authoring checklist:\n\n  Line 178: | SKILLED | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py --root <changed-scope>`; plus targeted language/project tests such as vitest, pytest, shellcheck, JSON validation, or spec validation for changed spec folders |\n\n  Line 179: | UNKNOWN | User-selected verification command set before completion claim |\n\n  Line 183: ## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n\n  Line 192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n\n  Line 194: ### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n\n  Line 198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n\n  Line 234: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n\n  Line 240: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n\n  Line 284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n\n  Line 429: # DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n\n  Line 444: - the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble (stack/phase detection and the universal quality baseline), plus\n\n  Line 449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n\n  Line 450: - Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n\n  Line 451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n\n  Line 462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n\n  Line 468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n\n  Line 470: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n\n  Line 477: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 8 stays the only replay source.\n\n  Line 479: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n\n  Line 481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n\n  Line 483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n\n  Line 486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n\n  Line 489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n\n  Line 490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n\n  Line 494: Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n\n  Line 495: The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n\n  Line 507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n\n  Line 512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n\n  Line 513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n\n  Line 520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n\n  Line 529: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n\n  Line 559:             \"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\",\n\n  Line 561:             \"disambiguation_checklist\": UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST,\n\n  Line 568:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"unknown_mode\": workflow_mode, \"resources\": loaded}\n\n  Line 580:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"notice\": \"matched registry but no packet SKILL.md was available\", \"resources\": loaded}\n","title":"grep","metadata":{"metadata":{"matches":53,"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Found 53 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeeste