{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527411102,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b383e001qnPpI3ql7CpX3c","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b2f22001BXE7FhtpQxaq19","type":"tool","id":"call_170a34b5d640499baec376b6","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md, lines 1-133\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Implementation\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the mutating build phase, from research before a change to the handoff into quality and verification.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code implementation workflow\"\n6:   - \"implement workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"mutating build workflow\"\n8:   - \"surface implementation standards\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.1.6.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Implementation\n15: \n16: Open this when you are about to change behavior and need to know how much to read before the first edit.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Implementation is the one lifecycle phase that builds new behavior. It reads before it writes, lands the smallest correct edit and hands the result on with the facts later phases need. It takes the active surface from the shared router and loads only that surface's resources.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Writing, modifying, refactoring or building code in the active surface.\n29: - Implementing a feature, script, component, module, fallback, parameter, authoring packet or runtime behavior.\n30: - Researching unfamiliar, risky, config-sensitive, security-sensitive or multi-file code before changing it.\n31: - Applying surface authoring patterns once the shared router has resolved the surface.\n32: - Using a library or framework overlay as peer reference after surface detection, never as a replacement for surface standards.\n33: \n34: ### When Not to Use\n35: \n36: - A concrete failing symptom dominates the task. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n37: - The code is written and needs author-side P0/P1/P2 checks, comment hygiene or surface checklists. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode, which runs `quality-gate.cjs` first.\n38: - The task is final non-mutating evidence. Use [verification](./workflow-verify.md).\n39: - The user wants findings-first review output instead of workspace edits. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n40: - The change is documentation-only prose with no executable behavior or routing change.\n41: \n42: ### Core Principle\n43: \n44: Read first, change the least and write the baseline down before the first edit. Debugging and verification can only measure a change against the starting state this phase recorded.\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n49: \n50: ### Research\n51: \n52: Research comes before any change to unfamiliar or high-blast code. For a small local edit it can be brief, but it always includes reading the target file.\n53: \n54: 1. Read every target file in this session before editing it.\n55: 2. List every place the change must reach (callers, tests, fixtures, config and exports). Read them, with nearby conventions and existing examples, before introducing a new shape.\n56: 3. Use the surface the shared router resolved ([stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md)). Never re-detect or override it inside this phase.\n57: 4. Load the smallest active-surface resource set that covers the intent and the changed file types.\n58: 5. For broad or risky work, run a bounded read-only research sweep before editing.\n59: 6. When design or other upstream guidance handed off locked values, constraints or risks, keep them as given instead of redesigning the task.\n60: \n61: ### Pre-Write Restraint\n62: \n63: Before the first edit of non-trivial work, record two facts:\n64: \n65: | Field | Content |\n66: | --- | --- |\n67: | Baseline | The starting state, in the shape of the Baseline row in the verification [Baseline And Delta](./workflow-verify.md#baseline-and-delta) table. Write `UNKNOWN` when no safe baseline exists, because verification then has to settle for a narrower claim. |\n68: | Blast Radius | One phrase, such as `low-blast, reversible`, `medium-blast: multi-file behavior` or `high-blast: touches auth/data/filesystem/config`. |\n69: \n70: Then walk the [Design Restraint Ladder](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md#design-restraint-ladder-pre-write) and stop at the first rung that holds. The ladder never cuts a P0 item or anything the user asked for. When requested scope looks unnecessary or risky, implement the requirement and raise a scope-amendment recommendation in the same response. Never cut scope silently. The restraint floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md` sections 1 and 2 and the Restraint Signals table in `AGENTS.md` section 3.\n71: \n72: ### Write\n73: \n74: 1. Write the smallest correct change that meets the request and the active-surface standards.\n75: 2. Keep the project's existing conventions unless the request changes them.\n76: 3. Stay inside the user's scope and the established documentation scope.\n77: 4. Keep comments durable and explanatory. Never add artifact labels or temporary process markers, because they go stale the moment the work that named them closes.\n78: 5. Keep routing metadata, config shapes, generated-metadata ownership and packet boundaries intact when authoring system assets.\n79: 6. Prepare the handoff: changed scope, baseline, likely checks and accepted residual risks.\n80: \n81: ### OpenCode Implementation Guardrails\n82: \n83: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [implementation guardrails](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#2-implementation-guardrails).\n84: \n85: ---\n86: \n87: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n88: \n89: ### Always\n90: \n91: - Always read a target file before editing it, including skeletons and existing README or contract files.\n92: - Always use the surface identity the shared router resolved before loading implementation resources.\n93: - Always pick the active-surface language or file-type guidance before applying standards.\n94: - Always load authoring checklists at write time when the active surface requires them.\n95: - Always record a baseline and a one-phrase blast-radius read for non-trivial work.\n96: - Always build the simplest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n97: - Always hand off to quality and verification explicitly once the code is written.\n98: \n99: ### Never\n100: \n101: - Never claim completion from implementation alone. The [Iron Law](./workflow-verify.md#iron-law) gates every completion claim.\n102: - Never treat an unsupported or ambiguous surface as supported.\n103: - Never apply one surface's standards to another surface's runtime behavior.\n104: - Never narrow requested scope silently because part of it looks unnecessary.\n105: - Never add packet-local advisor metadata when the parent owns the identity.\n106: - Never paste whole reference documents into the workflow. Link or load the focused reference instead.\n107: \n108: ### Escalate If\n109: \n110: - Surface identity stays ambiguous after the shared-router inputs are read.\n111: - Implementation evidence conflicts with the approved spec or a user instruction.\n112: - The verification command set is unknown and the user expects a completion claim.\n113: - Security-sensitive, filesystem, credential, destructive or production-data behavior is unclear.\n114: \n115: ---\n116: \n117: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n118: \n119: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the change for author-side checks, comment hygiene and active-surface checklists. The mode runs `quality-gate.cjs` first and loads every checklist its verdict lists.\n120: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives failing checks or runtime symptoms and traces one root cause at a time.\n121: - [Verification](./workflow-verify.md) receives the final changed state, the baseline, the commands to run and the exact claim to prove.\n122: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns findings-first critique when the user asks for severity-ranked risks instead of edits.\n123: \n124: The handoff is ready when the surface is known, the target files were read, the loaded resources match the active surface, non-trivial work carries a baseline and a blast-radius note and the change is the smallest correct implementation of the stated requirement.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n129: \n130: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that takes over when a symptom appears.\n131: - [workflow-verify.md](./workflow-verify.md) - the evidence phase, the Iron Law and the baseline shape this phase records.\n132: - [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md) - how a request maps to this phase and what each phase hands on.\n133: - [code-quality-standards.md](../../shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md) - the Design Restraint Ladder and the P0 items it never cuts."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-implement.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527410238,"end":1794527411102}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527411105,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b3a12001NKBj9VHZduj0ne","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b2f22001BXE7FhtpQxaq19","type":"tool","id":"call_709c138b4c474c6eaa698a7f","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md, lines 1-467\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Style Guide\n3: description: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n6:   - \"javascript file header format\"\n7:   - \"use strict directive\"\n8:   - \"javascript section organization\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Style Guide\n15: \n16: Open this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Defines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: Use when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n29: \n30: ### Use Cases\n31: \n32: - Writing new JavaScript files\n33: - Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n34: - Resolving style disagreements in code review\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n39: \n40: All JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\n41: using the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\n42: boundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\n43: follows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\n44: loader contract.\n45: \n46: ### Template\n47: \n48: ```javascript\n49: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n50: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n51: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### Requirements\n55: \n56: - Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n57: - Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n58: - A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n59: - Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n60:   this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n61: - `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n62:   `.js` requirement above\n63: - The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n64:   introduce it in new files\n65: \n66: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n67: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n72: \n73: Every `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\n74: This repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\n75: redundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\n76: files; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n77: \n78: ```javascript\n79: 'use strict';\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Placement**: Immediately after file header, before any other code.\n83: \n84: **Evidence**: `verify_alignment_drift.py` skips `.mjs`; CommonJS-style JS\n85: helpers still use `'use strict'`.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 4. SECTION ORGANIZATION\n90: \n91: Large files are organized using numbered section dividers.\n92: \n93: ### Section Divider Template\n94: \n95: ```javascript\n96: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // 1. [SECTION NAME]\n98: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ### Standard Section Order\n102: \n103: | Order | Section Name     | Purpose                           |\n104: |-------|------------------|-----------------------------------|\n105: | 1     | IMPORTS          | Module dependencies               |\n106: | 2     | CONSTANTS        | Configuration values, magic numbers |\n107: | 3     | HELPERS          | Internal utility functions        |\n108: | 4     | CORE LOGIC       | Main implementation               |\n109: | 5     | EXPORTS          | Module public interface           |\n110: \n111: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts`\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 5. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n116: \n117: ### Function Names\n118: \n119: **Style**: `camelCase`\n120: \n121: ```javascript\n122: // CORRECT\n123: function loadConfig(path) { }\n124: function memorySearch(query, options) { }\n125: function validateInput(data) { }\n126: \n127: // INCORRECT\n128: function load_config(path) { }     // snake_case\n129: function LoadConfig(path) { }      // PascalCase\n130: function load-config(path) { }     // kebab-case (invalid syntax)\n131: ```\n132: \n133: **Evidence**: MDN Web Docs, Airbnb Style Guide, Node.js core APIs\n134: \n135: ### Constant Names\n136: \n137: **Style**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`\n138: \n139: ```javascript\n140: // CORRECT\n141: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n142: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n143: const SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT = 30000;\n144: \n145: // INCORRECT\n146: const maxRetries = 3;       // camelCase\n147: const max_retries = 3;      // snake_case (reserved for variables)\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/core/config.ts`\n151: \n152: ### Class Names\n153: \n154: **Style**: `PascalCase`\n155: \n156: ```javascript\n157: // CORRECT\n158: class SpecFolderError extends Error { }\n159: class ConfigLoader { }\n160: class TriggerIndex { }\n161: \n162: // INCORRECT\n163: class specFolderError { }   // camelCase\n164: class spec_folder_error { } // snake_case\n165: ```\n166: \n167: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n168: \n169: ### Variable Names\n170: \n171: | Scope        | Style        | Example                          |\n172: |--------------|--------------|----------------------------------|\n173: | Local        | `camelCase`  | `const searchResults = []`       |\n174: | Module-level | `camelCase`  | `const dbPath = '...'`           |\n175: | Parameters   | `camelCase`  | `function search(query, maxResults)` |\n176: \n177: ### Boolean Names\n178: \n179: **Style**: `camelCase` with `is`/`has`/`can`/`should` prefix\n180: \n181: ```javascript\n182: // CORRECT\n183: const isValid = true;\n184: const hasResults = items.length > 0;\n185: const canProceed = !isBlocked;\n186: const shouldRetry = attempts < MAX_RETRIES;\n187: \n188: // INCORRECT\n189: const is_valid = true;       // snake_case\n190: const valid = true;          // ambiguous\n191: ```\n192: \n193: ### Naming Summary Table\n194: \n195: | Element          | Convention         | Example                |\n196: |------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n197: | Functions        | `camelCase`        | `loadConfig`           |\n198: | Constants        | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES`          |\n199: | Classes          | `PascalCase`       | `MemoryError`          |\n200: | Local variables  | `camelCase`        | `searchResults`        |\n201: | Module variables | `camelCase`        | `dbConnection`         |\n202: | Parameters       | `camelCase`        | `queryText`            |\n203: | Booleans         | `is`/`has`/`can`   | `isValid`, `hasItems`  |\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## 6. FORMATTING RULES\n208: \n209: ### Indentation\n210: \n211: - **Size**: 2 spaces\n212: - **Tabs**: Never use tabs\n213: \n214: ```javascript\n215: // CORRECT (2 spaces)\n216: function example() {\n217:   if (condition) {\n218:     return value;\n219:   }\n220: }\n221: \n222: // INCORRECT (4 spaces or tabs)\n223: function example() {\n224:     if (condition) {\n225:         return value;\n226:     }\n227: }\n228: ```\n229: \n230: ### Braces\n231: \n232: **Style**: K&R (opening brace on same line)\n233: \n234: ```javascript\n235: // CORRECT (K&R style)\n236: if (condition) {\n237:   // code\n238: } else {\n239:   // code\n240: }\n241: \n242: function example() {\n243:   // code\n244: }\n245: \n246: // INCORRECT (Allman style)\n247: if (condition)\n248: {\n249:   // code\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ### Semicolons\n254: \n255: **Rule**: Always use semicolons\n256: \n257: ```javascript\n258: // CORRECT\n259: const value = 42;\n260: return result;\n261: \n262: // INCORRECT (ASI-dependent)\n263: const value = 42\n264: return result\n265: ```\n266: \n267: ### Quotes\n268: \n269: **Rule**: Single quotes for strings\n270: \n271: ```javascript\n272: // CORRECT\n273: const message = 'Hello world';\n274: const template = `Value: ${value}`;  // Template literals OK\n275: \n276: // INCORRECT\n277: const message = \"Hello world\";  // Double quotes\n278: ```\n279: \n280: ### Line Length\n281: \n282: - **Soft limit**: roughly 100 to 120 characters, per the shared formatting rule\n283: - **Preferred**: 80 characters\n284: - **Exception**: URLs and long strings\n285: \n286: ---\n287: \n288: ## 7. COMMENTING RULES\n289: \n290: ### Principles\n291: \n292: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code, this surface's budget for the shared comment-density rule (see [naming and commenting](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md))\n293: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n294: 3. **Focus on WHY, not WHAT:** Explain intent, constraints, reasoning\n295: 4. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n296: \n297: ### Comment Examples\n298: \n299: Use plain comments that explain reasoning:\n300: \n301: - `// Reasoning for non-obvious choice`\n302: - `// Precondition/invariant check`\n303: - `// State that must always hold`\n304: - `// SEC: security note tied to a stable standard (CWE-###)`\n305: - `// Safety, performance, or reliability constraint`\n306: \n307: ### Capitalization\n308: \n309: All comment text MUST start with a capital letter:\n310: \n311: ```javascript\n312: // Reverse order preserves dependency chain     // correct\n313: // reverse order preserves dependency chain     // wrong\n314: ```\n315: \n316: **Exceptions**: `eslint-disable` directives, `@ts-` annotations, and inline code references.\n317: \n318: ### Reference Comments\n319: \n320: Use a bracketed module prefix for inline comments:\n321: \n322: ```javascript\n323: // [module-name] Description of what this does\n324: ```\n325: \n326: Cite only durable references, never a spec-folder-internal id. Comments must not embed a spec folder/number, task/checklist/requirement id (`T###`, `CHK-###`, `REQ-###`), feature-catalog entry, ADR id, or ticket id; see [`../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md) §4 and [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\".\n327: \n328: ```javascript\n329: // GOOD — durable WHY, no ephemeral id\n330: // Causal Memory Graph handlers: drift-why, causal-link, causal-stats, causal-unlink\n331: // Recover pending writes on startup so a crash cannot lose data\n332: // SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)\n333: ```\n334: \n335: ### Function Purpose Comments\n336: \n337: Single line above function describing intent:\n338: \n339: ```javascript\n340: // Load configuration from YAML file, merging with defaults\n341: // Returns validated config object or throws on invalid schema\n342: function loadConfig(configPath) { }\n343: \n344: // Calculate weighted memory score using decay function\n345: // Higher scores indicate more recent and relevant memories\n346: function calculateMemoryScore(memory, currentTime) { }\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Inline Comments (WHY, Not WHAT)\n350: \n351: **Good examples (explain reasoning):**\n352: \n353: ```javascript\n354: // Skip if already initialized to prevent double-binding\n355: if (window[INIT_FLAG]) return;\n356: \n357: // Sort by recency so newest memories surface first\n358: results.sort((a, b) => b.timestamp - a.timestamp);\n359: \n360: // Add timeout to prevent hang on unresponsive database\n361: const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>\n362:   setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timeout')), 30000)\n363: );\n364: \n365: // Close DB connection on all control paths\n366: try {\n367:   await processBatch(items);\n368: } finally {\n369:   db.close();\n370: }\n371: ```\n372: \n373: **Bad examples (narrate implementation):**\n374: \n375: ```javascript\n376: // Set the value to 42\n377: const value = 42;\n378: \n379: // Loop through items\n380: for (const item of items) { }\n381: \n382: // Check if result exists\n383: if (result) { }\n384: \n385: // Add item to array\n386: items.push(item);\n387: ```\n388: \n389: ### Debug Logging Pattern\n390: \n391: ```javascript\n392: // Conditional logging for debug mode\n393: const DEBUG = false;\n394: const LOG_PREFIX = '[ModuleName]';\n395: \n396: function log(...args) {\n397:   if (DEBUG) {\n398:     console.log(LOG_PREFIX, ...args);\n399:   }\n400: }\n401: \n402: // Usage\n403: log('Initialized with config:', config);\n404: log('Processing batch:', batch.length, 'items');\n405: ```\n406: \n407: ### JSDoc Comments\n408: \n409: Required for all exported functions. See `quality-standards.md` for full JSDoc format.\n410: \n411: ```javascript\n412: /**\n413:  * Brief description of function purpose.\n414:  *\n415:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n416:  * @param {Object} options - Search options\n417:  * @param {number} options.limit - Maximum results to return\n418:  * @returns {Array} Array of search results\n419:  */\n420: function search(query, options = {}) {\n421:   // implementation\n422: }\n423: ```\n424: \n425: ### TODO Comments\n426: \n427: Format for tracking incomplete work.\n428: \n429: ```javascript\n430: // TODO: [description of work needed]\n431: // FIXME: [description of bug to fix]\n432: ```\n433: \n434: ---\n435: \n436: ## 8. IMPORT ORDER\n437: \n438: Organize imports in three groups with blank lines between.\n439: \n440: ```javascript\n441: 'use strict';\n442: \n443: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n444: // 1. IMPORTS\n445: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n446: \n447: // Node.js built-ins\n448: const fs = require('fs');\n449: const path = require('path');\n450: \n451: // Third-party modules\n452: const yaml = require('js-yaml');\n453: const sqlite = require('better-sqlite3');\n454: \n455: // Local modules\n456: const { loadConfig } = require('./config');\n457: const logger = require('../utils/logger');\n458: ```\n459: \n460: **Evidence**: `mcp-server/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts:1-26`\n461: \n462: ---\n463: \n464: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n465: \n466: - [quality-standards.md](quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md) - Error handling, JSDoc, security patterns\n467: - [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Copy-paste templates and cheat sheets","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md, lines 1-467\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Style Guide\n3: description: Formatting standards and naming conventions for JavaScript files in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript style guide\"\n6:   - \"javascript file header format\"\n7:   - \"use strict directive\"\n8:   - \"javascript section organization\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.4.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Style Guide\n15: \n16: Open this when you are drafting or reviewing a JavaScript file and need the header, section order, naming rule or formatting detail that settles the point.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Defines consistent styling rules for JavaScript files to ensure readability, maintainability, and alignment across all OpenCode JavaScript code.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: Use when writing, reviewing, or resolving style questions for JavaScript files.\n29: \n30: ### Use Cases\n31: \n32: - Writing new JavaScript files\n33: - Reviewing JavaScript code for consistency\n34: - Resolving style disagreements in code review\n35: \n36: ---\n37: \n38: ## 2. FILE HEADER FORMAT\n39: \n40: All JavaScript files MUST begin with a module header identifying the module,\n41: using the divider form below. Module syntax follows the nearest package\n42: boundary and loader contract: `.cjs` is CommonJS, `.mjs` is ESM, and `.js`\n43: follows the nearest package's `type`. OpenCode plugin paths use their ESM\n44: loader contract.\n45: \n46: ### Template\n47: \n48: ```javascript\n49: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n50: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n51: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### Requirements\n55: \n56: - Divider width: 67 box-drawing characters after `// `\n57: - Module name: after `MODULE:` on the middle line\n58: - A shebang line may precede the header in an executable script\n59: - Immediately followed by `'use strict';` for `.js/.cjs` files, as required by\n60:   this repository's alignment checks; it is redundant in ESM `.js` files\n61: - `.mjs` files do not require `'use strict'`; ESM `.js` plugin files follow the\n62:   `.js` requirement above\n63: - The older 78-character `╔═╗` box is accepted where it already exists; do not\n64:   introduce it in new files\n65: \n66: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs:1-4`,\n67: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/retrieval/generate-trigger-index.mjs:1-4`\n68: \n69: ---\n70: \n71: ## 3. USE STRICT DIRECTIVE\n72: \n73: Every `.js` and `.cjs` JavaScript file MUST include the strict mode directive.\n74: This repository convention also applies to ESM `.js` files, where it is\n75: redundant because modules are strict by definition. Do not add it to `.mjs`\n76: files; the alignment verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` enforcement.\n77: \n78: ```javascript\n79: 'use strict';\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Placement**: Immediately after file header, before any other code.\n83: \n84: **Evidence**: `verify_alignment_drift.py` skips `.mjs`; CommonJS-style JS\n85: helpers still use `'use strict'`.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 4. SECTION ORGANIZATION\n90: \n91: Large files are organized using numbered section dividers.\n92: \n93: ### Section Divider Template\n94: \n95: ```javascript\n96: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n97: // 1. [SECTION NAME]\n98: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n99: ```\n100: \n101: ### Standard Section Order\n102: \n103: | Order | Section Name     | Purpose                           |\n104: |-------|------------------|-----------------------------------|\n105: | 1     | IMPORTS          | Module dependencies               |\n106: | 2     | CONSTANTS        | Configuration values, magic numbers |\n107: | 3     | HELPERS          | Internal utility functions        |\n108: | 4     | CORE LOGIC       | Main implementation               |\n109: | 5     | EXPORTS          | Module public interface           |\n110: \n111: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/core/config.ts`\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 5. NAMING CONVENTIONS\n116: \n117: ### Function Names\n118: \n119: **Style**: `camelCase`\n120: \n121: ```javascript\n122: // CORRECT\n123: function loadConfig(path) { }\n124: function memorySearch(query, options) { }\n125: function validateInput(data) { }\n126: \n127: // INCORRECT\n128: function load_config(path) { }     // snake_case\n129: function LoadConfig(path) { }      // PascalCase\n130: function load-config(path) { }     // kebab-case (invalid syntax)\n131: ```\n132: \n133: **Evidence**: MDN Web Docs, Airbnb Style Guide, Node.js core APIs\n134: \n135: ### Constant Names\n136: \n137: **Style**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`\n138: \n139: ```javascript\n140: // CORRECT\n141: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n142: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n143: const SQLITE_BUSY_TIMEOUT = 30000;\n144: \n145: // INCORRECT\n146: const maxRetries = 3;       // camelCase\n147: const max_retries = 3;      // snake_case (reserved for variables)\n148: ```\n149: \n150: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/core/config.ts`\n151: \n152: ### Class Names\n153: \n154: **Style**: `PascalCase`\n155: \n156: ```javascript\n157: // CORRECT\n158: class SpecFolderError extends Error { }\n159: class ConfigLoader { }\n160: class TriggerIndex { }\n161: \n162: // INCORRECT\n163: class specFolderError { }   // camelCase\n164: class spec_folder_error { } // snake_case\n165: ```\n166: \n167: **Evidence**: `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/lib/config/spec-doc-paths.ts`\n168: \n169: ### Variable Names\n170: \n171: | Scope        | Style        | Example                          |\n172: |--------------|--------------|----------------------------------|\n173: | Local        | `camelCase`  | `const searchResults = []`       |\n174: | Module-level | `camelCase`  | `const dbPath = '...'`           |\n175: | Parameters   | `camelCase`  | `function search(query, maxResults)` |\n176: \n177: ### Boolean Names\n178: \n179: **Style**: `camelCase` with `is`/`has`/`can`/`should` prefix\n180: \n181: ```javascript\n182: // CORRECT\n183: const isValid = true;\n184: const hasResults = items.length > 0;\n185: const canProceed = !isBlocked;\n186: const shouldRetry = attempts < MAX_RETRIES;\n187: \n188: // INCORRECT\n189: const is_valid = true;       // snake_case\n190: const valid = true;          // ambiguous\n191: ```\n192: \n193: ### Naming Summary Table\n194: \n195: | Element          | Convention         | Example                |\n196: |------------------|--------------------|------------------------|\n197: | Functions        | `camelCase`        | `loadConfig`           |\n198: | Constants        | `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_RETRIES`          |\n199: | Classes          | `PascalCase`       | `MemoryError`          |\n200: | Local variables  | `camelCase`        | `searchResults`        |\n201: | Module variables | `camelCase`        | `dbConnection`         |\n202: | Parameters       | `camelCase`        | `queryText`            |\n203: | Booleans         | `is`/`has`/`can`   | `isValid`, `hasItems`  |\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## 6. FORMATTING RULES\n208: \n209: ### Indentation\n210: \n211: - **Size**: 2 spaces\n212: - **Tabs**: Never use tabs\n213: \n214: ```javascript\n215: // CORRECT (2 spaces)\n216: function example() {\n217:   if (condition) {\n218:     return value;\n219:   }\n220: }\n221: \n222: // INCORRECT (4 spaces or tabs)\n223: function example() {\n224:     if (condition) {\n225:         return value;\n226:     }\n227: }\n228: ```\n229: \n230: ### Braces\n231: \n232: **Style**: K&R (opening brace on same line)\n233: \n234: ```javascript\n235: // CORRECT (K&R style)\n236: if (condition) {\n237:   // code\n238: } else {\n239:   // code\n240: }\n241: \n242: function example() {\n243:   // code\n244: }\n245: \n246: // INCORRECT (Allman style)\n247: if (condition)\n248: {\n249:   // code\n250: }\n251: ```\n252: \n253: ### Semicolons\n254: \n255: **Rule**: Always use semicolons\n256: \n257: ```javascript\n258: // CORRECT\n259: const value = 42;\n260: return result;\n261: \n262: // INCORRECT (ASI-dependent)\n263: const value = 42\n264: return result\n265: ```\n266: \n267: ### Quotes\n268: \n269: **Rule**: Single quotes for strings\n270: \n271: ```javascript\n272: // CORRECT\n273: const message = 'Hello world';\n274: const template = `Value: ${value}`;  // Template literals OK\n275: \n276: // INCORRECT\n277: const message = \"Hello world\";  // Double quotes\n278: ```\n279: \n280: ### Line Length\n281: \n282: - **Soft limit**: roughly 100 to 120 characters, per the shared formatting rule\n283: - **Preferred**: 80 characters\n284: - **Exception**: URLs and long strings\n285: \n286: ---\n287: \n288: ## 7. COMMENTING RULES\n289: \n290: ### Principles\n291: \n292: 1. **Quantity limit (Skilled surface setting):** Maximum 3 comments per 10 lines of code, this surface's budget for the shared comment-density rule (see [naming and commenting](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md))\n293: 2. **Focus on purposeful semantics:** Explain WHY something is done, not WHAT it does\n294: 3. **Focus on WHY, not WHAT:** Explain intent, constraints, reasoning\n295: 4. **No commented-out code:** Delete unused code (git preserves history)\n296: \n297: ### Comment Examples\n298: \n299: Use plain comments that explain reasoning:\n300: \n301: - `// Reasoning for non-obvious choice`\n302: - `// Precondition/invariant check`\n303: - `// State that must always hold`\n304: - `// SEC: security note tied to a stable standard (CWE-###)`\n305: - `// Safety, performance, or reliability constraint`\n306: \n307: ### Capitalization\n308: \n309: All comment text MUST start with a capital letter:\n310: \n311: ```javascript\n312: // Reverse order preserves dependency chain     // correct\n313: // reverse order preserves dependency chain     // wrong\n314: ```\n315: \n316: **Exceptions**: `eslint-disable` directives, `@ts-` annotations, and inline code references.\n317: \n318: ### Reference Comments\n319: \n320: Use a bracketed module prefix for inline comments:\n321: \n322: ```javascript\n323: // [module-name] Description of what this does\n324: ```\n325: \n326: Cite only durable references, never a spec-folder-internal id. Comments must not embed a spec folder/number, task/checklist/requirement id (`T###`, `CHK-###`, `REQ-###`), feature-catalog entry, ADR id, or ticket id; see [`../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`](../shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md) §4 and [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) §4 \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\".\n327: \n328: ```javascript\n329: // GOOD — durable WHY, no ephemeral id\n330: // Causal Memory Graph handlers: drift-why, causal-link, causal-stats, causal-unlink\n331: // Recover pending writes on startup so a crash cannot lose data\n332: // SEC: sanitize input to prevent stored XSS (CWE-79)\n333: ```\n334: \n335: ### Function Purpose Comments\n336: \n337: Single line above function describing intent:\n338: \n339: ```javascript\n340: // Load configuration from YAML file, merging with defaults\n341: // Returns validated config object or throws on invalid schema\n342: function loadConfig(configPath) { }\n343: \n344: // Calculate weighted memory score using decay function\n345: // Higher scores indicate more recent and relevant memories\n346: function calculateMemoryScore(memory, currentTime) { }\n347: ```\n348: \n349: ### Inline Comments (WHY, Not WHAT)\n350: \n351: **Good examples (explain reasoning):**\n352: \n353: ```javascript\n354: // Skip if already initialized to prevent double-binding\n355: if (window[INIT_FLAG]) return;\n356: \n357: // Sort by recency so newest memories surface first\n358: results.sort((a, b) => b.timestamp - a.timestamp);\n359: \n360: // Add timeout to prevent hang on unresponsive database\n361: const timeout = new Promise((_, reject) =>\n362:   setTimeout(() => reject(new Error('Timeout')), 30000)\n363: );\n364: \n365: // Close DB connection on all control paths\n366: try {\n367:   await processBatch(items);\n368: } finally {\n369:   db.close();\n370: }\n371: ```\n372: \n373: **Bad examples (narrate implementation):**\n374: \n375: ```javascript\n376: // Set the value to 42\n377: const value = 42;\n378: \n379: // Loop through items\n380: for (const item of items) { }\n381: \n382: // Check if result exists\n383: if (result) { }\n384: \n385: // Add item to array\n386: items.push(item);\n387: ```\n388: \n389: ### Debug Logging Pattern\n390: \n391: ```javascript\n392: // Conditional logging for debug mode\n393: const DEBUG = false;\n394: const LOG_PREFIX = '[ModuleName]';\n395: \n396: function log(...args) {\n397:   if (DEBUG) {\n398:     console.log(LOG_PREFIX, ...args);\n399:   }\n400: }\n401: \n402: // Usage\n403: log('Initialized with config:', config);\n404: log('Processing batch:', batch.length, 'items');\n405: ```\n406: \n407: ### JSDoc Comments\n408: \n409: Required for all exported functions. See `quality-standards.md` for full JSDoc format.\n410: \n411: ```javascript\n412: /**\n413:  * Brief description of function purpose.\n414:  *\n415:  * @param {string} query - Search query text\n416:  * @param {Object} options - Search options\n417:  * @param {number} options.limit - Maximum results to return\n418:  * @returns {Array} Array of search results\n419:  */\n420: function search(query, options = {}) {\n421:   // implementation\n422: }\n423: ```\n424: \n425: ### TODO Comments\n426: \n427: Format for tracking incomplete work.\n428: \n429: ```javascript\n430: // TODO: [description of work needed]\n431: // FIXME: [description of bug to fix]\n432: ```\n433: \n434: ---\n435: \n436: ## 8. IMPORT ORDER\n437: \n438: Organize imports in three groups with blank lines between.\n439: \n440: ```javascript\n441: 'use strict';\n442: \n443: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n444: // 1. IMPORTS\n445: // ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n446: \n447: // Node.js built-ins\n448: const fs = require('fs');\n449: const path = require('path');\n450: \n451: // Third-party modules\n452: const yaml = require('js-yaml');\n453: const sqlite = require('better-sqlite3');\n454: \n455: // Local modules\n456: const { loadConfig } = require('./config');\n457: const logger = require('../utils/logger');\n458: ```\n459: \n460: **Evidence**: `mcp-server/lib/search/folder-discovery.ts:1-26`\n461: \n462: ---\n463: \n464: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n465: \n466: - [quality-standards.md](quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md) - Error handling, JSDoc, security patterns\n467: - [quick-reference.md](./quick-reference.md) - Copy-paste templates and cheat sheets"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527410706,"end":1794527411105}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527411107,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b36a3002Cz0NrDtChsXrZ7","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b2f22001BXE7FhtpQxaq19","type":"tool","id":"call_575a33442c55427ea64e59ce","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md, lines 1-213\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Code Quality Standards\n3: description: Stack-agnostic P0/P1/P2 severity model for the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"universal code quality standards\"\n6:   - \"p0 p1 p2 severity tiers\"\n7:   - \"code quality gate severity\"\n8:   - \"stack agnostic quality contract\"\n9:   - \"design restraint ladder\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 1.6.5.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Universal Code Quality Standards\n16: \n17: Open this when a review finding needs a severity tier, or before the first write, to check the Design Restraint Ladder.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: The severity contract behind every surface quality checklist in `sk-code`. P0 blocks completion. P1 needs explicit approval to defer. P2 is tracked and never blocks. Surface checklists hold the concrete rules and give each one a tier from this contract.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - At the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate, which the `sk-code-quality` mode runs before Phase 1 (Implementation) can be called complete.\n30: - When authoring or reviewing a surface quality checklist.\n31: - When deciding whether a finding blocks merge or can ship as a follow-up.\n32: - When the `sk-code-review` mode ranks findings, so reviewer and author tiers mean the same thing.\n33: \n34: ### Core Principle\n35: \n36: Severity tiers exist so reviewers and authors agree on what blocks \"done\". A rule's tier is a stable contract: the bar does not move per PR, per author or per deadline.\n37: \n38: ### Key Sources\n39: \n40: - Surface checklists: the checklists each surface declares. For this repository's own code that is SKILLED, under `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/`.\n41: - Rule ids, the hard floor and overrides: [`rule-overrides.md`](./rule-overrides.md) and [`rule-overrides.json`](./rule-overrides.json).\n42: - Findings-first review baseline: the `sk-code-review` mode and its review output contract.\n43: \n44: ### Design Restraint Ladder (pre-write)\n45: \n46: Before writing NEW code for an implementation task, stop at the first rung that holds. Each rung down adds code someone must read, test and keep. The ladder runs AFTER surface and intent routing, so its vocabulary matches the detected surface. It is a post-read reflex: rungs 2 to 5 need you to read what already exists.\n47: \n48: 1. **Does this need to exist at all? (YAGNI)** If the requirement looks unnecessary, recommend a scope amendment in the same response. NEVER silently cut scope. SCOPE LOCK and the Logic-Sync HALT still apply.\n49: 2. **Already in this codebase (a helper, component, service, pattern)?** Use it the way the surrounding code does.\n50: 3. **Standard library / language built-in?**\n51: 4. **Native platform or runtime feature?** Use the native API of the detected surface.\n52: 5. **An already-installed dependency?** Never add a new dependency for what a few lines do.\n53: 6. **Can it be one line?**\n54: 7. **Only then: the minimum code that works.**\n55: \n56: Restraint never cuts a P0 item in §3 below, such as input validation, error handling, secrets handling and accessibility, or anything the user asked for.\n57: \n58: The ladder consumes the detected surface and does NOT change surface precedence. sk-code-skilled is the fallback surface, always last, and any other declared surface outranks it. Nor does the ladder change the Iron Law: Phase 3 verification is still required. For the over-engineering, gold-plating and scope-creep detectors, see the Restraint Signals table in the repository's `AGENTS.md` and the restraint rules in its `.skilled/repo-rules/`, such as `prevent-overengineering.md`, when present.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. SEVERITY TIERS\n63: \n64: The Code Quality Gate puts every quality rule in exactly one tier:\n65: \n66: | Tier   | Handling      | Completion Impact                               |\n67: | ------ | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n68: | **P0** | HARD BLOCKER  | Cannot claim done until fixed                   |\n69: | **P1** | Required      | Must fix OR get user approval to defer          |\n70: | **P2** | Optional      | Can defer with a documented reason. Non-blocking |\n71: \n72: **Gate rule**: if ANY P0 item fails, completion is BLOCKED until fixed. P1 violations need explicit deferral approval. P2 violations are tracked but do not block.\n73: \n74: Review ranks findings on its own scale, so the review-severity column in sections 3 to 5 maps each rule onto the ids in section 2 of [`review-core.md`](../../../sk-code-review/references/review-core.md). An author can then see how a finding reads in review before review runs. Review keeps its ids, their meanings and its judgement on each finding, so the column is a forecast and never overrides a reviewer.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 3. P0 CATEGORIES (universal, apply across stacks)\n79: \n80: The universal floor is sections 3 to 5 of this file. Every surface gets it, UNKNOWN included. It holds only rules that hold in any codebase: house style, such as doc-comment forms, typing strictness and naming schemes, belongs to the surface that chose it.\n81: \n82: ### Rule ids and overrides\n83: \n84: Every floor rule carries an id in the `<class>.<name>` grammar of the [rule overrides contract](./rule-overrides.md) section 3, and an Override mark:\n85: \n86: - **FIXED** rules are the hard floor in section 4 of that contract and in [`rule-overrides.json`](./rule-overrides.json). No source changes them, and a `## Rule overrides` section that names one is refused.\n87: - **overridable** rules are the default. A repository or surface may set one to `none` or replace it in a `## Rule overrides` section of a checklist file its declaration names, as section 6 of the contract allows. Without such a row the rule applies as written here.\n88: \n89: When more than one source speaks to the same rule, the contract's precedence in section 5 decides: the hard floor, then the repository checklist, then a specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then the rule as this file ships it. A surface checklist may still add rules of its own.\n90: \n91: ### Mechanical Floor (every surface)\n92: \n93: Three faults are P0 on every surface, because no other check can be trusted on a file that has one.\n94: \n95: - The quality runner's built-in checks detect a parse failure in JSON, in JavaScript through both its CommonJS and ESM loaders, in Python, in shell, in YAML and in TOML.\n96: - The YAML check parses through PyYAML and the TOML check through `tomllib`, and each runs only when the `python3` in use imports its parser. When one cannot, that check reads `SKIPPED_ABSENT` with a `repair` naming what to install, and the run stays verified because neither check is required.\n97: - They also detect the conflict markers and the credential signature over every readable text file.\n98: - A parse check for any language outside that list stays with the surface checklist or a configured `syntax-*` check, so no surface restates what the runner already covers.\n99: \n100: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n101: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n102: | `syntax.*` | FIXED | **Parse failure**: the file does not parse in its language, which a shebang decides before the extension. A finding in plain JavaScript is heuristic and does not block, because a `.js` file may hold JSX or Flow, and a finding in a `.json` file that parses only once its comments and trailing commas are stripped is heuristic too, because the tool that reads it may accept them. The agent confirms either finding first. | P1, once a heuristic finding is confirmed |\n103: | `conflict.*` | FIXED | **Merge-conflict markers**: a text file holds the lines of an unresolved merge conflict. | P1 |\n104: | `secret.*` | FIXED | **Credential signature**: a file holds a private-key header or a token in a known credential format. | P0 |\n105: \n106: ### Categories\n107: \n108: P0 covers issues that have caused production incidents or reviewer-author confusion:\n109: \n110: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n111: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n112: | `floor.initialization-safety` | FIXED | **Initialization safety**: code that sets up state guards against double initialization, race conditions and partial loads. | P1. P0 when the gap is exploitable or causes data loss |\n113: | `floor.input-validation` | FIXED | **Input validation**: every external input (function parameters, API responses, file contents, env vars) is validated at the boundary. | P1. P0 when the gap is exploitable or causes data loss |\n114: | `floor.commented-out-code` | overridable | **No commented-out code**: delete it. Commented code rots and misleads readers. | P1 |\n115: | `floor.silent-failures` | FIXED | **No silent failures**: an exception either reaches the caller or is logged with enough context to debug it. | P1 |\n116: | `floor.hardcoded-secrets` | FIXED | **No hardcoded secrets**: credentials, API keys and tokens never sit inline. They come from env vars or a secret store. | P0 |\n117: | `floor.ephemeral-pointers` | overridable | **No ephemeral-artifact pointers in comments**: a comment never names a spec folder, a packet, phase, task, checklist or requirement id, a feature-catalog entry, an ADR id or a ticket id. Those get renamed or archived and leave a dangling pointer. Keep the durable WHY. The full contract is \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\" in `code-style-guide.md` §4. | P1 |\n118: | `floor.accessibility` | FIXED | **Accessibility** (user-facing UI): interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state. | P1 |\n119: \n120: `floor.ephemeral-pointers` is the judgment half of a rule the runner also checks. Its machine half is the comment-hygiene checker, whose findings carry the `hygiene.*` ids of `runner-contract.md` section 5, such as `hygiene.issue`, `hygiene.task`, `hygiene.adr` and `hygiene.spec-path`. Under the contract's machine-checked rule, a `none` row for `floor.ephemeral-pointers` or for one `hygiene.*` id changes what the agent and review apply, and the runner's own check changes only through `commentHygiene.disableRules` in the repository's quality config. A `replace:` row never reaches the runner's check.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. P1 CATEGORIES (universal)\n125: \n126: P1 covers issues that degrade maintainability or reliability:\n127: \n128: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n129: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n130: | `floor.boundary-tests` | overridable | **Test coverage at boundaries**: happy path plus at least one edge case per public surface. | P1 |\n131: | `floor.error-messages` | overridable | **Error message quality**: every error carries enough context for the operator to act. | P2 |\n132: | `floor.resource-cleanup` | FIXED | **Resource cleanup**: file handles, network connections, subscriptions, observers and timers close or cancel deterministically. | P1 |\n133: | `floor.type-safety` | overridable | **Type checks pass**: where the repository runs a type checker, the change passes it. How strict the types must be is the surface's rule. | P1 when the type check fails, P2 otherwise |\n134: | `floor.naming` | overridable | **Naming convention adherence**: names follow the convention the repository or its surface declares. | P2 |\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. P2 CATEGORIES (universal)\n139: \n140: P2 covers issues that improve quality but do not affect correctness or maintainability:\n141: \n142: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n143: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n144: | `floor.documentation` | overridable | **Documentation where it earns its place**: a non-obvious contract or a complex algorithm carries a comment that explains WHY. Whether public APIs carry doc comments, and in which form, is the surface's rule. | P2 |\n145: | `floor.idiomatic` | overridable | **Idiomatic refinements**: code follows community style guides and project conventions. | P2 |\n146: | `floor.performance-polish` | overridable | **Performance polish**: optimization beyond the NFR targets. | P2 |\n147: | `floor.extra-tests` | overridable | **Test coverage beyond boundaries**: extra edge cases, fuzz tests and property tests. | P2 |\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 6. SURFACE-SPECIFIC CHECKLISTS\n152: \n153: Each surface adds its own rules to these universal tiers and gives each rule a tier. This file is the contract, and the surface checklists are the implementations. A surface checklist may also carry a `## Rule overrides` section for an overridable floor id, under the precedence in section 3.\n154: \n155: | Surface | Phase 1.5 source | Notes |\n156: | ------- | ---------------- | ----- |\n157: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/` | The fallback. The SKILLED house checklist, `universal-checklist.md`, plus one per touched language (JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, JSON/JSONC), the JavaScript and CSS house style under `js-css-style/` and authoring checklists for agents, commands, skills and MCP servers. It carries the doc-comment, typing and naming detail this floor leaves to the surface |\n158: | CI | `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | Rule index for GitHub Actions workflows |\n159: | Frontend | None. `sk-code-frontend` ships no checklist | Web and Figma-to-code practice as read-only references. A file it covers gets this universal floor through `sk-code-quality`, plus the SKILLED or declared surface its path resolves to |\n160: | Declared specific surface | The standards its skill ships | Declared through `surface_detection` in that skill's `graph-metadata.json`. Outranks SKILLED |\n161: | UNKNOWN | The universal floor in sections 3 to 5, the quality runner's built-in checks and the checks and checklists the repository's quality config names, each with its overrides applied | sk-code does not own Go, React/Next.js server code, generic Node.js, React Native or Swift. Apply those, then ask for the surface and its commands. The spec-folder authoring checklist applies only where the SKILLED surface resolves, and no discovered command applies |\n162: \n163: ---\n164: \n165: ## 7. WORKFLOW\n166: \n167: At the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate:\n168: \n169: 1. Identify the file type and code surface. The smart router does this, and `../stack-detection.md` holds the detection order.\n170: 2. Run the quality runner on the changed files before committing:\n171:    ```bash\n172:    node .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs\n173:    ```\n174:    It reads the working-tree change by default, or `--files <path>...`, and prints one verdict. Its built-in checks cover the mechanical floor in §3, and the comment-hygiene checker is one of its sensors, so one run checks both. Zero blocking findings are required. `BLOCKED` stops completion until the finding is fixed, and `UNVERIFIED` or `NOTHING_CHECKED` never reads as clean. To suppress a known comment-hygiene false positive, put `hygiene-ok` on that comment line. The gate values and the verdict fields are in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md`.\n175: \n176:    A repository may also enforce comment hygiene outside the runner: at write time, at commit time and in CI. The verdict's `layers` field reports what a probe found for each of those layers, and a quality claim cites that field. This repository's own wiring is listed under \"Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\" in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`.\n177: \n178: 3. Load the matching surface checklist from §6.\n179: 4. Check every P0 item and fix each failure before going on.\n180: 5. Check every P1 item. Fix it or record the approved deferral.\n181: 6. Check every P2 item and track misses for follow-up. They do not block.\n182: 7. Mark each checklist item `[x]` with evidence: the code that passes the rule or the test that proves it.\n183: 8. Go to Phase 2 (Debugging) when a check fails, otherwise to Phase 3 (Verification).\n184: \n185: ---\n186: \n187: ## 8. RELATIONSHIP TO `sk-code`'s CODE-REVIEW MODE\n188: \n189: The `sk-code-quality` mode produces **surface compliance evidence** at Phase 1.5, where the author checks their own change. The `sk-code-review` mode owns the **findings-first review output**: severity-ranked findings, risk reporting and the security and correctness minimums.\n190: \n191: Use `sk-code-review` when:\n192: \n193: - A PR or a stakeholder needs a structured, severity-ranked findings report.\n194: - Security or correctness minimums need an audit.\n195: \n196: Use `sk-code-quality` when:\n197: \n198: - You are the author checking your own implementation before claiming done.\n199: - You need surface-specific quality rules during development.\n200: \n201: Both modes rank against these tiers, so author evidence and review findings add up to one quality story.\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n206: \n207: - [`./rule-overrides.md`](./rule-overrides.md) - the override contract: the rule id grammar, the hard floor, precedence and how an override is recorded.\n208: - [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) - language-agnostic naming, formatting and structure principles.\n209: - [`../stack-detection.md`](../stack-detection.md) - surface detection order, with specific surfaces first and the SKILLED fallback last.\n210: - [`../workflow-implement.md`](../workflow-implement.md) - Phase 1, where the Design Restraint Ladder is applied.\n211: - [`../workflow-debug.md`](../workflow-debug.md) - root-cause analysis and command-failure recovery when Phase 1.5 fails and the fix is not obvious.\n212: - [`../workflow-verify.md`](../workflow-verify.md) - the pre-claim gate that runs after Phase 1.5 clears, before any \"done\" claim.\n213: - [`../phase-detection.md`](../phase-detection.md) - Phase 1.5 position in the sk-code lifecycle.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md, lines 1-213\n1: ---\n2: title: Universal Code Quality Standards\n3: description: Stack-agnostic P0/P1/P2 severity model for the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"universal code quality standards\"\n6:   - \"p0 p1 p2 severity tiers\"\n7:   - \"code quality gate severity\"\n8:   - \"stack agnostic quality contract\"\n9:   - \"design restraint ladder\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 1.6.5.0\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Universal Code Quality Standards\n16: \n17: Open this when a review finding needs a severity tier, or before the first write, to check the Design Restraint Ladder.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: The severity contract behind every surface quality checklist in `sk-code`. P0 blocks completion. P1 needs explicit approval to defer. P2 is tracked and never blocks. Surface checklists hold the concrete rules and give each one a tier from this contract.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - At the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate, which the `sk-code-quality` mode runs before Phase 1 (Implementation) can be called complete.\n30: - When authoring or reviewing a surface quality checklist.\n31: - When deciding whether a finding blocks merge or can ship as a follow-up.\n32: - When the `sk-code-review` mode ranks findings, so reviewer and author tiers mean the same thing.\n33: \n34: ### Core Principle\n35: \n36: Severity tiers exist so reviewers and authors agree on what blocks \"done\". A rule's tier is a stable contract: the bar does not move per PR, per author or per deadline.\n37: \n38: ### Key Sources\n39: \n40: - Surface checklists: the checklists each surface declares. For this repository's own code that is SKILLED, under `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/`.\n41: - Rule ids, the hard floor and overrides: [`rule-overrides.md`](./rule-overrides.md) and [`rule-overrides.json`](./rule-overrides.json).\n42: - Findings-first review baseline: the `sk-code-review` mode and its review output contract.\n43: \n44: ### Design Restraint Ladder (pre-write)\n45: \n46: Before writing NEW code for an implementation task, stop at the first rung that holds. Each rung down adds code someone must read, test and keep. The ladder runs AFTER surface and intent routing, so its vocabulary matches the detected surface. It is a post-read reflex: rungs 2 to 5 need you to read what already exists.\n47: \n48: 1. **Does this need to exist at all? (YAGNI)** If the requirement looks unnecessary, recommend a scope amendment in the same response. NEVER silently cut scope. SCOPE LOCK and the Logic-Sync HALT still apply.\n49: 2. **Already in this codebase (a helper, component, service, pattern)?** Use it the way the surrounding code does.\n50: 3. **Standard library / language built-in?**\n51: 4. **Native platform or runtime feature?** Use the native API of the detected surface.\n52: 5. **An already-installed dependency?** Never add a new dependency for what a few lines do.\n53: 6. **Can it be one line?**\n54: 7. **Only then: the minimum code that works.**\n55: \n56: Restraint never cuts a P0 item in §3 below, such as input validation, error handling, secrets handling and accessibility, or anything the user asked for.\n57: \n58: The ladder consumes the detected surface and does NOT change surface precedence. sk-code-skilled is the fallback surface, always last, and any other declared surface outranks it. Nor does the ladder change the Iron Law: Phase 3 verification is still required. For the over-engineering, gold-plating and scope-creep detectors, see the Restraint Signals table in the repository's `AGENTS.md` and the restraint rules in its `.skilled/repo-rules/`, such as `prevent-overengineering.md`, when present.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 2. SEVERITY TIERS\n63: \n64: The Code Quality Gate puts every quality rule in exactly one tier:\n65: \n66: | Tier   | Handling      | Completion Impact                               |\n67: | ------ | ------------- | ----------------------------------------------- |\n68: | **P0** | HARD BLOCKER  | Cannot claim done until fixed                   |\n69: | **P1** | Required      | Must fix OR get user approval to defer          |\n70: | **P2** | Optional      | Can defer with a documented reason. Non-blocking |\n71: \n72: **Gate rule**: if ANY P0 item fails, completion is BLOCKED until fixed. P1 violations need explicit deferral approval. P2 violations are tracked but do not block.\n73: \n74: Review ranks findings on its own scale, so the review-severity column in sections 3 to 5 maps each rule onto the ids in section 2 of [`review-core.md`](../../../sk-code-review/references/review-core.md). An author can then see how a finding reads in review before review runs. Review keeps its ids, their meanings and its judgement on each finding, so the column is a forecast and never overrides a reviewer.\n75: \n76: ---\n77: \n78: ## 3. P0 CATEGORIES (universal, apply across stacks)\n79: \n80: The universal floor is sections 3 to 5 of this file. Every surface gets it, UNKNOWN included. It holds only rules that hold in any codebase: house style, such as doc-comment forms, typing strictness and naming schemes, belongs to the surface that chose it.\n81: \n82: ### Rule ids and overrides\n83: \n84: Every floor rule carries an id in the `<class>.<name>` grammar of the [rule overrides contract](./rule-overrides.md) section 3, and an Override mark:\n85: \n86: - **FIXED** rules are the hard floor in section 4 of that contract and in [`rule-overrides.json`](./rule-overrides.json). No source changes them, and a `## Rule overrides` section that names one is refused.\n87: - **overridable** rules are the default. A repository or surface may set one to `none` or replace it in a `## Rule overrides` section of a checklist file its declaration names, as section 6 of the contract allows. Without such a row the rule applies as written here.\n88: \n89: When more than one source speaks to the same rule, the contract's precedence in section 5 decides: the hard floor, then the repository checklist, then a specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then the rule as this file ships it. A surface checklist may still add rules of its own.\n90: \n91: ### Mechanical Floor (every surface)\n92: \n93: Three faults are P0 on every surface, because no other check can be trusted on a file that has one.\n94: \n95: - The quality runner's built-in checks detect a parse failure in JSON, in JavaScript through both its CommonJS and ESM loaders, in Python, in shell, in YAML and in TOML.\n96: - The YAML check parses through PyYAML and the TOML check through `tomllib`, and each runs only when the `python3` in use imports its parser. When one cannot, that check reads `SKIPPED_ABSENT` with a `repair` naming what to install, and the run stays verified because neither check is required.\n97: - They also detect the conflict markers and the credential signature over every readable text file.\n98: - A parse check for any language outside that list stays with the surface checklist or a configured `syntax-*` check, so no surface restates what the runner already covers.\n99: \n100: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n101: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n102: | `syntax.*` | FIXED | **Parse failure**: the file does not parse in its language, which a shebang decides before the extension. A finding in plain JavaScript is heuristic and does not block, because a `.js` file may hold JSX or Flow, and a finding in a `.json` file that parses only once its comments and trailing commas are stripped is heuristic too, because the tool that reads it may accept them. The agent confirms either finding first. | P1, once a heuristic finding is confirmed |\n103: | `conflict.*` | FIXED | **Merge-conflict markers**: a text file holds the lines of an unresolved merge conflict. | P1 |\n104: | `secret.*` | FIXED | **Credential signature**: a file holds a private-key header or a token in a known credential format. | P0 |\n105: \n106: ### Categories\n107: \n108: P0 covers issues that have caused production incidents or reviewer-author confusion:\n109: \n110: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n111: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n112: | `floor.initialization-safety` | FIXED | **Initialization safety**: code that sets up state guards against double initialization, race conditions and partial loads. | P1. P0 when the gap is exploitable or causes data loss |\n113: | `floor.input-validation` | FIXED | **Input validation**: every external input (function parameters, API responses, file contents, env vars) is validated at the boundary. | P1. P0 when the gap is exploitable or causes data loss |\n114: | `floor.commented-out-code` | overridable | **No commented-out code**: delete it. Commented code rots and misleads readers. | P1 |\n115: | `floor.silent-failures` | FIXED | **No silent failures**: an exception either reaches the caller or is logged with enough context to debug it. | P1 |\n116: | `floor.hardcoded-secrets` | FIXED | **No hardcoded secrets**: credentials, API keys and tokens never sit inline. They come from env vars or a secret store. | P0 |\n117: | `floor.ephemeral-pointers` | overridable | **No ephemeral-artifact pointers in comments**: a comment never names a spec folder, a packet, phase, task, checklist or requirement id, a feature-catalog entry, an ADR id or a ticket id. Those get renamed or archived and leave a dangling pointer. Keep the durable WHY. The full contract is \"No ephemeral-artifact pointers\" in `code-style-guide.md` §4. | P1 |\n118: | `floor.accessibility` | FIXED | **Accessibility** (user-facing UI): interactive elements stay keyboard-operable, carry an accessible name and keep a visible focus state. | P1 |\n119: \n120: `floor.ephemeral-pointers` is the judgment half of a rule the runner also checks. Its machine half is the comment-hygiene checker, whose findings carry the `hygiene.*` ids of `runner-contract.md` section 5, such as `hygiene.issue`, `hygiene.task`, `hygiene.adr` and `hygiene.spec-path`. Under the contract's machine-checked rule, a `none` row for `floor.ephemeral-pointers` or for one `hygiene.*` id changes what the agent and review apply, and the runner's own check changes only through `commentHygiene.disableRules` in the repository's quality config. A `replace:` row never reaches the runner's check.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 4. P1 CATEGORIES (universal)\n125: \n126: P1 covers issues that degrade maintainability or reliability:\n127: \n128: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n129: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n130: | `floor.boundary-tests` | overridable | **Test coverage at boundaries**: happy path plus at least one edge case per public surface. | P1 |\n131: | `floor.error-messages` | overridable | **Error message quality**: every error carries enough context for the operator to act. | P2 |\n132: | `floor.resource-cleanup` | FIXED | **Resource cleanup**: file handles, network connections, subscriptions, observers and timers close or cancel deterministically. | P1 |\n133: | `floor.type-safety` | overridable | **Type checks pass**: where the repository runs a type checker, the change passes it. How strict the types must be is the surface's rule. | P1 when the type check fails, P2 otherwise |\n134: | `floor.naming` | overridable | **Naming convention adherence**: names follow the convention the repository or its surface declares. | P2 |\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 5. P2 CATEGORIES (universal)\n139: \n140: P2 covers issues that improve quality but do not affect correctness or maintainability:\n141: \n142: | Id | Override | Rule | Review severity |\n143: | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n144: | `floor.documentation` | overridable | **Documentation where it earns its place**: a non-obvious contract or a complex algorithm carries a comment that explains WHY. Whether public APIs carry doc comments, and in which form, is the surface's rule. | P2 |\n145: | `floor.idiomatic` | overridable | **Idiomatic refinements**: code follows community style guides and project conventions. | P2 |\n146: | `floor.performance-polish` | overridable | **Performance polish**: optimization beyond the NFR targets. | P2 |\n147: | `floor.extra-tests` | overridable | **Test coverage beyond boundaries**: extra edge cases, fuzz tests and property tests. | P2 |\n148: \n149: ---\n150: \n151: ## 6. SURFACE-SPECIFIC CHECKLISTS\n152: \n153: Each surface adds its own rules to these universal tiers and gives each rule a tier. This file is the contract, and the surface checklists are the implementations. A surface checklist may also carry a `## Rule overrides` section for an overridable floor id, under the precedence in section 3.\n154: \n155: | Surface | Phase 1.5 source | Notes |\n156: | ------- | ---------------- | ----- |\n157: | SKILLED | `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/` | The fallback. The SKILLED house checklist, `universal-checklist.md`, plus one per touched language (JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, JSON/JSONC), the JavaScript and CSS house style under `js-css-style/` and authoring checklists for agents, commands, skills and MCP servers. It carries the doc-comment, typing and naming detail this floor leaves to the surface |\n158: | CI | `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | Rule index for GitHub Actions workflows |\n159: | Frontend | None. `sk-code-frontend` ships no checklist | Web and Figma-to-code practice as read-only references. A file it covers gets this universal floor through `sk-code-quality`, plus the SKILLED or declared surface its path resolves to |\n160: | Declared specific surface | The standards its skill ships | Declared through `surface_detection` in that skill's `graph-metadata.json`. Outranks SKILLED |\n161: | UNKNOWN | The universal floor in sections 3 to 5, the quality runner's built-in checks and the checks and checklists the repository's quality config names, each with its overrides applied | sk-code does not own Go, React/Next.js server code, generic Node.js, React Native or Swift. Apply those, then ask for the surface and its commands. The spec-folder authoring checklist applies only where the SKILLED surface resolves, and no discovered command applies |\n162: \n163: ---\n164: \n165: ## 7. WORKFLOW\n166: \n167: At the Phase 1.5 Code Quality Gate:\n168: \n169: 1. Identify the file type and code surface. The smart router does this, and `../stack-detection.md` holds the detection order.\n170: 2. Run the quality runner on the changed files before committing:\n171:    ```bash\n172:    node .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/scripts/quality-gate.cjs\n173:    ```\n174:    It reads the working-tree change by default, or `--files <path>...`, and prints one verdict. Its built-in checks cover the mechanical floor in §3, and the comment-hygiene checker is one of its sensors, so one run checks both. Zero blocking findings are required. `BLOCKED` stops completion until the finding is fixed, and `UNVERIFIED` or `NOTHING_CHECKED` never reads as clean. To suppress a known comment-hygiene false positive, put `hygiene-ok` on that comment line. The gate values and the verdict fields are in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md`.\n175: \n176:    A repository may also enforce comment hygiene outside the runner: at write time, at commit time and in CI. The verdict's `layers` field reports what a probe found for each of those layers, and a quality claim cites that field. This repository's own wiring is listed under \"Comment-Hygiene Enforcement Gates\" in `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/SKILL.md`.\n177: \n178: 3. Load the matching surface checklist from §6.\n179: 4. Check every P0 item and fix each failure before going on.\n180: 5. Check every P1 item. Fix it or record the approved deferral.\n181: 6. Check every P2 item and track misses for follow-up. They do not block.\n182: 7. Mark each checklist item `[x]` with evidence: the code that passes the rule or the test that proves it.\n183: 8. Go to Phase 2 (Debugging) when a check fails, otherwise to Phase 3 (Verification).\n184: \n185: ---\n186: \n187: ## 8. RELATIONSHIP TO `sk-code`'s CODE-REVIEW MODE\n188: \n189: The `sk-code-quality` mode produces **surface compliance evidence** at Phase 1.5, where the author checks their own change. The `sk-code-review` mode owns the **findings-first review output**: severity-ranked findings, risk reporting and the security and correctness minimums.\n190: \n191: Use `sk-code-review` when:\n192: \n193: - A PR or a stakeholder needs a structured, severity-ranked findings report.\n194: - Security or correctness minimums need an audit.\n195: \n196: Use `sk-code-quality` when:\n197: \n198: - You are the author checking your own implementation before claiming done.\n199: - You need surface-specific quality rules during development.\n200: \n201: Both modes rank against these tiers, so author evidence and review findings add up to one quality story.\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 9. RELATED RESOURCES\n206: \n207: - [`./rule-overrides.md`](./rule-overrides.md) - the override contract: the rule id grammar, the hard floor, precedence and how an override is recorded.\n208: - [`../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md`](../../../sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md) - language-agnostic naming, formatting and structure principles.\n209: - [`../stack-detection.md`](../stack-detection.md) - surface detection order, with specific surfaces first and the SKILLED fallback last.\n210: - [`../workflow-implement.md`](../workflow-implement.md) - Phase 1, where the Design Restraint Ladder is applied.\n211: - [`../workflow-debug.md`](../workflow-debug.md) - root-cause analysis and command-failure recovery when Phase 1.5 fails and the fix is not obvious.\n212: - [`../workflow-verify.md`](../workflow-verify.md) - the pre-claim gate that runs after Phase 1.5 clears, before any \"done\" claim.\n213: - [`../phase-detection.md`](../phase-detection.md) - Phase 1.5 position in the sk-code lifecycle."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527409827,"end":1794527411107}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527411110,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b3914001BclRtsU8zm313X","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b2f22001BXE7FhtpQxaq19","type":"tool","id":"call_532e6ff3434741d1bb50ddb8","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md, lines 1-177\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Verification\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n6:   - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"falsifier discipline\"\n8:   - \"completion claim gate\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.2.7.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Verification\n15: \n16: Open this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Verification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n29: - Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n30: - Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n31: - Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n32: - Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n33: - Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n34: \n35: ### When Not to Use\n36: \n37: - The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n38: - The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n39: - Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n40: - The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n41: - The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n42: \n43: ### Iron Law\n44: \n45: **Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n46: \n47: A clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n52: \n53: ### Verification Loop\n54: \n55: 1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n56: 2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n57:    - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n58:    - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n59:    - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n60:    - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n61:    - The run verdict has three readers: this step, the @code RETURN `Quality Gate` field and `sk-code-review`, which reads it as input evidence only.\n62: 3. Name the claim to prove and the command, action or observation that proves it.\n63: 4. Select the surface-appropriate command set and targeted tests from the active-surface guidance.\n64: 5. Run fresh commands and capture the command names, exit codes, test counts, important output and failures.\n65: 6. Add end-to-end, runtime or performance evidence when the claim depends on behavior outside static checks.\n66: 7. Compare the current evidence to the baseline and report the delta.\n67: 8. Run a mutation check or claim falsifier when the claim depends on a new or changed test.\n68: 9. Walk the Pre-Claim Gate below, then state only the claims the evidence proves. If the evidence fails, hand back without editing.\n69: \n70: ### Baseline And Delta\n71: \n72: Verification compares against the baseline whenever it states a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n73: \n74: | Field | Required Content |\n75: | --- | --- |\n76: | Baseline | Starting command status, failing tests, warning count, runtime issue, performance number or `UNKNOWN` when no baseline was captured. |\n77: | Current | Fresh output from the whole gate, not only the subset you iterated on: exit codes, test counts, runtime observations or measured numbers. |\n78: | Delta | What changed, improved, regressed or remains unproven, including the parts that did not move. |\n79: | Claim Scope | The exact claim the evidence supports and the blind spot that remains. |\n80: \n81: No baseline means no broad no-regressions claim. A narrower claim tied to fresh evidence still stands.\n82: \n83: The baseline floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 5 (BASELINES). This table is the sk-code record shape for it. Implementation records its Baseline row before the first edit.\n84: \n85: ### Verification Ladder\n86: \n87: Climb from the cheapest rung to the most authoritative and name what each rung cannot see:\n88: \n89: | Rung | Proves | Blind Spot |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | Unit | Isolated logic or assertion path | Integration, wiring, real I/O, runtime state |\n92: | In-memory | Multiple modules without external runtime | Real file system, serialization, deployed config, external state |\n93: | On-server | Runtime service or local server path | Deployment differences, caches, real user environment |\n94: | Live | Exact exercised path in real target | Unexercised paths, other users, other environments, future data |\n95: \n96: ### Mutation Check And Claim Falsifier\n97: \n98: After green evidence for a new or modified test, confirm the test can fail for the right reason when that is feasible. A true red result is an assertion failing against the intended behavior. A compile error, or a command that never reached the assertion, is not a satisfying red. If a test stays green after the guarded behavior is broken, report it as a verification defect and hand back.\n99: \n100: This applies the idea behind `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 4 (THE NEGATIVE CONTROL): a check that passes whether or not the behavior works proves nothing. The before-fix half of that control, reproducing the failure with the same check, is step 2 of the [Debug Loop](./workflow-debug.md#debug-loop).\n101: \n102: ### Pre-Claim Gate Substance\n103: \n104: Walk every gate in order before any status claim:\n105: \n106: | Gate | Required Discipline |\n107: | --- | --- |\n108: | Identify | Name the command, action or observation that proves the claim. |\n109: | Run | Execute the surface-appropriate verification commands in the current session. |\n110: | Test | Exercise targeted unit, integration, end-to-end, runtime or manual checks where available, including at least one edge case and one error path. |\n111: | Verify | Confirm the output, exit code, test count and observed behavior match expectations, and that the run reached the changed code on a fresh build with real assertions. |\n112: | Record | Save the exact evidence the final claim rests on. |\n113: | Reread | Reread the request and its amendments, and confirm each outcome it asks for is backed by recorded evidence or named as not done. |\n114: | Claim | State only the narrow claim the evidence supports. |\n115: \n116: The Verify row guards against the four ways a green run lies, which `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 3 lists: it did not run, it ran on the wrong thing, it ran on stale artifacts or it asserted nothing.\n117: \n118: ### OpenCode Verification Reality\n119: \n120: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [verification reality](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#3-verification-reality), including the `validate.sh` pointer, and the [runtime build traps](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#4-runtime-build-traps).\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n125: \n126: ### Always\n127: \n128: - Always run fresh verification in the current session before a completion-related claim.\n129: - Always resolve surface identity before selecting commands.\n130: - Always record command names, exit codes, key output, test counts and runtime observations.\n131: - Always report the baseline, current result, delta and claim scope for a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n132: - Always name the verification ladder rung reached and the blind spot that remains.\n133: - Always include behavior evidence when runtime behavior changed and tooling is available.\n134: - Always hand back when verification finds a defect.\n135: - Always confirm the diff scope matches the stated change, with no accidental extras or debug residue.\n136: - Always compare warnings against the last working state, so a new warning is reported and not absorbed.\n137: \n138: ### Never\n139: \n140: - Never edit or create files during verification.\n141: - Never dispatch subagents from verification.\n142: - Never claim done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready from unrun commands.\n143: - Never claim no regressions without a baseline and a delta.\n144: - Never treat one green rung as proof of a higher rung.\n145: - Never hide a failed or skipped verifier behind a positive summary.\n146: - Never invent runtime, test or performance evidence.\n147: - Never verify against stale build output. Clean and rebuild first when an artifact is suspect.\n148: - Never present a check run in one environment as proof for every environment.\n149: \n150: ### Escalate If\n151: \n152: - The surface or the required verification command set is unknown.\n153: - A required verifier path is missing or unsafe to run.\n154: - Commands contradict each other on a blocking outcome.\n155: - Runtime evidence is required but unavailable and the claim would mislead without it.\n156: - The baseline is missing and the requested claim needs a regression comparison.\n157: - A mutation check shows the test is vacuous.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n162: \n163: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives reproducible verification failures, failed commands, broken runtime checks or vacuous tests.\n164: - [Implementation](./workflow-implement.md) receives the handback when required behavior is missing or build work is incomplete.\n165: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the handback when author-side checks, comment hygiene or checklist issues block verification.\n166: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns critique when the user wants findings instead of final evidence.\n167: \n168: Verification is ready to report only when it ran last, used only non-mutating actions, recorded fresh active-surface evidence, compared against the baseline when the claim needed it, named its blind spots and handed back any defect without editing.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n173: \n174: - [workflow-implement.md](./workflow-implement.md) - the build phase whose baseline and handoff this phase reads.\n175: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that receives every failed verification.\n176: - [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - surface detection, which decides the command set.\n177: - [workflow-guardrails.md](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md) - the verification command chain for system code under `.skilled/`.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md, lines 1-177\n1: ---\n2: title: Workflow Reference - Verification\n3: description: Shared sk-code doctrine for the final non-mutating evidence phase that gates every done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code verification workflow\"\n6:   - \"verify workflow doctrine\"\n7:   - \"falsifier discipline\"\n8:   - \"completion claim gate\"\n9: importance_tier: important\n10: contextType: general\n11: version: 2.2.7.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # Workflow Reference - Verification\n15: \n16: Open this before you claim a change is finished, and when you need a falsifier that could disprove it.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Verification owns evidence, not fixes. It runs fresh surface-appropriate commands, records the outcomes, compares them against the implementation baseline, names the blind spots and claims only what the evidence proves. When verification fails, it hands back to debugging, implementation or quality without editing.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Verifying work after implementation, quality checks or debugging.\n29: - Running final commands before a done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready claim.\n30: - Collecting evidence for active-surface behavior or system-code changes.\n31: - Reporting the delta from the baseline captured before implementation.\n32: - Running a mutation check or claim falsifier after a green result.\n33: - Walking the verification ladder and naming each rung's blind spot.\n34: \n35: ### When Not to Use\n36: \n37: - The task needs code written, files scaffolded or behavior changed. Use [implementation](./workflow-implement.md).\n38: - The task is author-side quality cleanup. Use the `sk-code-quality` mode.\n39: - Verification finds a defect that needs root-cause repair. Use [debugging](./workflow-debug.md).\n40: - The user wants findings-first review output or PR-style critique. Use the `sk-code-review` mode.\n41: - The active surface and verification command set are unknown. Ask for them before claiming anything.\n42: \n43: ### Iron Law\n44: \n45: **Iron Law**: no completion claim without fresh surface-appropriate verification evidence.\n46: \n47: A clean static read is not runtime evidence. A green unit test is not live proof. A successful build is not exercised behavior. One exercised path is not full regression coverage. Name the rung you reached and its blind spot.\n48: \n49: ---\n50: \n51: ## 2. WORKFLOW\n52: \n53: ### Verification Loop\n54: \n55: 1. Resolve the active surface through [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md). If it is unsupported or ambiguous, ask for the verification command set before claiming anything. The quality config, `repository/quality.json` in the `sk-code-quality` packet, names quality checks only, so it never supplies the verification command set.\n56: 2. Read the implementation, quality or debug handoff for the intended behavior, changed scope, baseline and accepted risks.\n57:    - From the quality envelope, read the `checker_outputs` field with the `gate` and `unverified` values it carries, and any field that still reads `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`.\n58:    - Hand back to quality only on `BLOCKED`, `ERROR` or a field still reading `PENDING_AGENT_JUDGMENT`, which is the 3 to 1.5 transition in [phase-detection.md](../../shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n59:    - On `UNVERIFIED`, prove each id it lists with this phase's own command or report that id open.\n60:    - On `NOTHING_CHECKED`, prove the claim with this phase's own commands and never read the gate as clean, because no check read any file as code, which is where a Markdown-only change lands.\n61:    - The run verdict has three readers: this step, the @code RETURN `Quality Gate` field and `sk-code-review`, which reads it as input evidence only.\n62: 3. Name the claim to prove and the command, action or observation that proves it.\n63: 4. Select the surface-appropriate command set and targeted tests from the active-surface guidance.\n64: 5. Run fresh commands and capture the command names, exit codes, test counts, important output and failures.\n65: 6. Add end-to-end, runtime or performance evidence when the claim depends on behavior outside static checks.\n66: 7. Compare the current evidence to the baseline and report the delta.\n67: 8. Run a mutation check or claim falsifier when the claim depends on a new or changed test.\n68: 9. Walk the Pre-Claim Gate below, then state only the claims the evidence proves. If the evidence fails, hand back without editing.\n69: \n70: ### Baseline And Delta\n71: \n72: Verification compares against the baseline whenever it states a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n73: \n74: | Field | Required Content |\n75: | --- | --- |\n76: | Baseline | Starting command status, failing tests, warning count, runtime issue, performance number or `UNKNOWN` when no baseline was captured. |\n77: | Current | Fresh output from the whole gate, not only the subset you iterated on: exit codes, test counts, runtime observations or measured numbers. |\n78: | Delta | What changed, improved, regressed or remains unproven, including the parts that did not move. |\n79: | Claim Scope | The exact claim the evidence supports and the blind spot that remains. |\n80: \n81: No baseline means no broad no-regressions claim. A narrower claim tied to fresh evidence still stands.\n82: \n83: The baseline floor belongs to `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 5 (BASELINES). This table is the sk-code record shape for it. Implementation records its Baseline row before the first edit.\n84: \n85: ### Verification Ladder\n86: \n87: Climb from the cheapest rung to the most authoritative and name what each rung cannot see:\n88: \n89: | Rung | Proves | Blind Spot |\n90: | --- | --- | --- |\n91: | Unit | Isolated logic or assertion path | Integration, wiring, real I/O, runtime state |\n92: | In-memory | Multiple modules without external runtime | Real file system, serialization, deployed config, external state |\n93: | On-server | Runtime service or local server path | Deployment differences, caches, real user environment |\n94: | Live | Exact exercised path in real target | Unexercised paths, other users, other environments, future data |\n95: \n96: ### Mutation Check And Claim Falsifier\n97: \n98: After green evidence for a new or modified test, confirm the test can fail for the right reason when that is feasible. A true red result is an assertion failing against the intended behavior. A compile error, or a command that never reached the assertion, is not a satisfying red. If a test stays green after the guarded behavior is broken, report it as a verification defect and hand back.\n99: \n100: This applies the idea behind `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 4 (THE NEGATIVE CONTROL): a check that passes whether or not the behavior works proves nothing. The before-fix half of that control, reproducing the failure with the same check, is step 2 of the [Debug Loop](./workflow-debug.md#debug-loop).\n101: \n102: ### Pre-Claim Gate Substance\n103: \n104: Walk every gate in order before any status claim:\n105: \n106: | Gate | Required Discipline |\n107: | --- | --- |\n108: | Identify | Name the command, action or observation that proves the claim. |\n109: | Run | Execute the surface-appropriate verification commands in the current session. |\n110: | Test | Exercise targeted unit, integration, end-to-end, runtime or manual checks where available, including at least one edge case and one error path. |\n111: | Verify | Confirm the output, exit code, test count and observed behavior match expectations, and that the run reached the changed code on a fresh build with real assertions. |\n112: | Record | Save the exact evidence the final claim rests on. |\n113: | Reread | Reread the request and its amendments, and confirm each outcome it asks for is backed by recorded evidence or named as not done. |\n114: | Claim | State only the narrow claim the evidence supports. |\n115: \n116: The Verify row guards against the four ways a green run lies, which `.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md` section 3 lists: it did not run, it ran on the wrong thing, it ran on stale artifacts or it asserted nothing.\n117: \n118: ### OpenCode Verification Reality\n119: \n120: For system code under `.skilled/` (the `sk-code-skilled` surface), also apply the [verification reality](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#3-verification-reality), including the `validate.sh` pointer, and the [runtime build traps](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md#4-runtime-build-traps).\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 3. DISCIPLINE\n125: \n126: ### Always\n127: \n128: - Always run fresh verification in the current session before a completion-related claim.\n129: - Always resolve surface identity before selecting commands.\n130: - Always record command names, exit codes, key output, test counts and runtime observations.\n131: - Always report the baseline, current result, delta and claim scope for a no-regressions or improvement claim.\n132: - Always name the verification ladder rung reached and the blind spot that remains.\n133: - Always include behavior evidence when runtime behavior changed and tooling is available.\n134: - Always hand back when verification finds a defect.\n135: - Always confirm the diff scope matches the stated change, with no accidental extras or debug residue.\n136: - Always compare warnings against the last working state, so a new warning is reported and not absorbed.\n137: \n138: ### Never\n139: \n140: - Never edit or create files during verification.\n141: - Never dispatch subagents from verification.\n142: - Never claim done, works, complete, fixed, passing or ready from unrun commands.\n143: - Never claim no regressions without a baseline and a delta.\n144: - Never treat one green rung as proof of a higher rung.\n145: - Never hide a failed or skipped verifier behind a positive summary.\n146: - Never invent runtime, test or performance evidence.\n147: - Never verify against stale build output. Clean and rebuild first when an artifact is suspect.\n148: - Never present a check run in one environment as proof for every environment.\n149: \n150: ### Escalate If\n151: \n152: - The surface or the required verification command set is unknown.\n153: - A required verifier path is missing or unsafe to run.\n154: - Commands contradict each other on a blocking outcome.\n155: - Runtime evidence is required but unavailable and the claim would mislead without it.\n156: - The baseline is missing and the requested claim needs a regression comparison.\n157: - A mutation check shows the test is vacuous.\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 4. HANDOFF BOUNDARIES\n162: \n163: - [Debugging](./workflow-debug.md) receives reproducible verification failures, failed commands, broken runtime checks or vacuous tests.\n164: - [Implementation](./workflow-implement.md) receives the handback when required behavior is missing or build work is incomplete.\n165: - Quality (`sk-code-quality`) receives the handback when author-side checks, comment hygiene or checklist issues block verification.\n166: - Review (`sk-code-review`) owns critique when the user wants findings instead of final evidence.\n167: \n168: Verification is ready to report only when it ran last, used only non-mutating actions, recorded fresh active-surface evidence, compared against the baseline when the claim needed it, named its blind spots and handed back any defect without editing.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 5. RELATED RESOURCES\n173: \n174: - [workflow-implement.md](./workflow-implement.md) - the build phase whose baseline and handoff this phase reads.\n175: - [workflow-debug.md](./workflow-debug.md) - the repair phase that receives every failed verification.\n176: - [stack-detection.md](../../shared/references/stack-detection.md) - surface detection, which decides the command set.\n177: - [workflow-guardrails.md](../../sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md) - the verification command chain for system code under `.skilled/`."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/shared/references/workflow-verify.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527410452,"end":1794527411110}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794527412476,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"id":"prt_1d22b40fc001NbsLIwJGZimjLT","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b2f22001BXE7FhtpQxaq19","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"cc1f3a0a44988f3275a2a713fb68a6a577f3fccc","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":1402,"output":279,"reasoning":73,"cache":{"read":91008,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794527414730,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"id":"prt_1d22b49ca001PploVeftSaW442","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b4101001n5ci4kUiqYLzE9","type":"step-start","snapshot":"1f10219f7e0ed91e3dc9318714771555f7d7dc1f"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527418150,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b54c900169hZcs2i5FuuVQ","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b4101001n5ci4kUiqYLzE9","type":"tool","id":"call_51ed602f099d4d4e90d5065e","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md, lines 1-71\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\"\n3: description: \"OpenCode-only rules that sit on top of the shared implement and verify workflow: the continuity writer, the sk-git boundary, the spec validation and typecheck chain, and runtime build traps.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode workflow guardrails\"\n6:   - \"opencode verification reality\"\n7:   - \"runtime build traps\"\n8:   - \"rebuild dist before verifying\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\n15: \n16: OpenCode-only rules for the implement and verify phases.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: The shared [implementation](../workflow-implement.md) and [verification](../workflow-verify.md) workflows apply on every surface. This file adds what holds only for OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`: the implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and the runtime build traps.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Implementing or verifying a change to OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`\n29: - Deciding whether a source edit needs a `dist/` rebuild before its behavior is verified\n30: - Handing missing build output, stale generated runtime files or a wrong package boundary back to implementation\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. IMPLEMENTATION GUARDRAILS\n35: \n36: - Treat `generate-context.js` as the single writer for a packet's continuity metadata, invoked through `/speckit:save`. It keeps atomic same-directory update and lock semantics, so do not hand-edit the generated metadata pair alongside it or run a second writer against the same packet. There is no index or embedding store behind it to mutate separately.\n37: - For git worktree isolation, defer to `sk-git`. This workflow may note that isolation is needed, but it must not duplicate `sk-git`'s worktree setup, branch, commit, or finish-work contract.\n38: - Preserve the verification handoff. Implementation should name the package boundary, rebuild requirement, baseline, likely test command, and any env knobs the verifier must pin. Final evidence belongs to [Workflow Reference - Verification](../workflow-verify.md), not implementation.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. VERIFICATION REALITY\n43: \n44: OpenCode verification starts with the real spec validation contract when a spec folder is in scope:\n45: \n46: ```bash\n47: bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The exit-code and warning contract of `validate.sh` is owned by `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md` (section 1 for the exit taxonomy, section 14 for the ways a run misleads). Read it there, not from a copy here. The one fact this workflow needs: a completion claim requires the explicit `RESULT: PASSED` line, because a warning never changes the exit code and exit status alone has misled in both directions.\n51: \n52: Use the package script for the package you changed:\n53: \n54: - **Spec-kit root and project-reference workspaces**: `tsc --build`.\n55: - **Satellite packages with their own package boundary**: `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`. A satellite typecheck script may add `--noEmit --composite false` over that same overlay.\n56: - **TypeScript tests**: run the package's Vitest-backed script where present, such as `npm test`, `npm run test:core`, or the targeted `vitest run ...` command exposed by that package.\n57: \n58: **Which suite a change owes.** Run the full suite of every package the change touches, and only those. A change under `system-spec-kit/runtime` owes `npm test` there. A change under `system-deep-loop/runtime` owes `npm test` there. CI runs the whole matrix after a push, so a local run of a package you did not touch proves nothing new.\n59: \n60: **When a rebase owes a rerun.** List what the rebase brought in with `git diff --stat <old base> <new base>`. When it brought only generated files, such as the trigger index or a packet's `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, the earlier run still stands. When it brought source changes to a package you touched, run that package's suite again.\n61: \n62: When verification discovers missing build output, stale generated runtime files, or a wrong package boundary, hand back to [Workflow Reference - Implementation](../workflow-implement.md) before making any completion claim.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 4. RUNTIME BUILD TRAPS\n67: \n68: - MCP servers, daemon-backed CLIs, and runtime hooks execute built `dist/` output. Editing a `.ts` source file has no runtime effect until the owning package rebuilds its `dist/` artifacts.\n69: - Rebuild before verifying behavior that depends on generated output. For `system-skill-advisor`, the server package names `dist/mcp-server/advisor-server.js` as the compiled backend artifact and `npm run build` as the command that builds TypeScript into `dist/`.\n70: - Keep env-sensitive tests deterministic. Set feature flags, provider choices, database paths, and timeout knobs explicitly in the command or test fixture. Record those values with the result. Do not rely on inherited shell state when the claim depends on a flag.\n71: - After a Node version change, run the native rebuild helper from the spec-kit root: `bash scripts/setup/rebuild-native-modules.sh`. It rebuilds native modules including `better-sqlite3` in `runtime/` and shared workspace modules, then records the new Node version marker.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md, lines 1-71\n1: ---\n2: title: \"OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\"\n3: description: \"OpenCode-only rules that sit on top of the shared implement and verify workflow: the continuity writer, the sk-git boundary, the spec validation and typecheck chain, and runtime build traps.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode workflow guardrails\"\n6:   - \"opencode verification reality\"\n7:   - \"runtime build traps\"\n8:   - \"rebuild dist before verifying\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.2.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # OpenCode Workflow Guardrails\n15: \n16: OpenCode-only rules for the implement and verify phases.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: The shared [implementation](../workflow-implement.md) and [verification](../workflow-verify.md) workflows apply on every surface. This file adds what holds only for OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`: the implementation guardrails, the verification command chain and the runtime build traps.\n25: \n26: ### When to Use\n27: \n28: - Implementing or verifying a change to OpenCode system code under `.skilled/`\n29: - Deciding whether a source edit needs a `dist/` rebuild before its behavior is verified\n30: - Handing missing build output, stale generated runtime files or a wrong package boundary back to implementation\n31: \n32: ---\n33: \n34: ## 2. IMPLEMENTATION GUARDRAILS\n35: \n36: - Treat `generate-context.js` as the single writer for a packet's continuity metadata, invoked through `/speckit:save`. It keeps atomic same-directory update and lock semantics, so do not hand-edit the generated metadata pair alongside it or run a second writer against the same packet. There is no index or embedding store behind it to mutate separately.\n37: - For git worktree isolation, defer to `sk-git`. This workflow may note that isolation is needed, but it must not duplicate `sk-git`'s worktree setup, branch, commit, or finish-work contract.\n38: - Preserve the verification handoff. Implementation should name the package boundary, rebuild requirement, baseline, likely test command, and any env knobs the verifier must pin. Final evidence belongs to [Workflow Reference - Verification](../workflow-verify.md), not implementation.\n39: \n40: ---\n41: \n42: ## 3. VERIFICATION REALITY\n43: \n44: OpenCode verification starts with the real spec validation contract when a spec folder is in scope:\n45: \n46: ```bash\n47: bash .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh <spec-folder> --strict\n48: ```\n49: \n50: The exit-code and warning contract of `validate.sh` is owned by `.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/references/validation/validation-rules.md` (section 1 for the exit taxonomy, section 14 for the ways a run misleads). Read it there, not from a copy here. The one fact this workflow needs: a completion claim requires the explicit `RESULT: PASSED` line, because a warning never changes the exit code and exit status alone has misled in both directions.\n51: \n52: Use the package script for the package you changed:\n53: \n54: - **Spec-kit root and project-reference workspaces**: `tsc --build`.\n55: - **Satellite packages with their own package boundary**: `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json`. A satellite typecheck script may add `--noEmit --composite false` over that same overlay.\n56: - **TypeScript tests**: run the package's Vitest-backed script where present, such as `npm test`, `npm run test:core`, or the targeted `vitest run ...` command exposed by that package.\n57: \n58: **Which suite a change owes.** Run the full suite of every package the change touches, and only those. A change under `system-spec-kit/runtime` owes `npm test` there. A change under `system-deep-loop/runtime` owes `npm test` there. CI runs the whole matrix after a push, so a local run of a package you did not touch proves nothing new.\n59: \n60: **When a rebase owes a rerun.** List what the rebase brought in with `git diff --stat <old base> <new base>`. When it brought only generated files, such as the trigger index or a packet's `description.json` and `graph-metadata.json`, the earlier run still stands. When it brought source changes to a package you touched, run that package's suite again.\n61: \n62: When verification discovers missing build output, stale generated runtime files, or a wrong package boundary, hand back to [Workflow Reference - Implementation](../workflow-implement.md) before making any completion claim.\n63: \n64: ---\n65: \n66: ## 4. RUNTIME BUILD TRAPS\n67: \n68: - MCP servers, daemon-backed CLIs, and runtime hooks execute built `dist/` output. Editing a `.ts` source file has no runtime effect until the owning package rebuilds its `dist/` artifacts.\n69: - Rebuild before verifying behavior that depends on generated output. For `system-skill-advisor`, the server package names `dist/mcp-server/advisor-server.js` as the compiled backend artifact and `npm run build` as the command that builds TypeScript into `dist/`.\n70: - Keep env-sensitive tests deterministic. Set feature flags, provider choices, database paths, and timeout knobs explicitly in the command or test fixture. Record those values with the result. Do not rely on inherited shell state when the claim depends on a flag.\n71: - After a Node version change, run the native rebuild helper from the spec-kit root: `bash scripts/setup/rebuild-native-modules.sh`. It rebuilds native modules including `better-sqlite3` in `runtime/` and shared workspace modules, then records the new Node version marker."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/references/shared/workflow-guardrails.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527417546,"end":1794527418150}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794527418153,"sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","part":{"partID":"prt_1d22b55e1001L3BkFjtir8k8Y2","sessionID":"ses_e2dd83989ffevoEHqeWn41PhPb","messageID":"msg_1d22b4101001n5ci4kUiqYLzE9","type":"tool","id":"call_99014374ebcf469cbc5cfe70","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md, lines 1-359\n1: ---\n2: title: JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n3: description: Quality validation checklist for JavaScript code in the OpenCode development environment.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"opencode javascript checklist\"\n6:   - \"javascript quality validation checklist\"\n7:   - \"js box header check\"\n8:   - \"use strict checklist\"\n9: importance_tier: normal\n10: contextType: implementation\n11: version: 1.0.5.0\n12: ---\n13: \n14: # JavaScript Code Quality Checklist\n15: \n16: Open this when a JavaScript file is ready for review and you want the quality checks specific to it. It runs alongside the universal checklist.\n17: \n18: ---\n19: \n20: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n21: \n22: ### Purpose\n23: \n24: Specific quality checks for JavaScript files. Use alongside the [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) for complete validation.\n25: \n26: ### Usage\n27: \n28: Apply these checks to JavaScript changes after completing the universal checklist, then use the priority levels below to determine which findings block completion.\n29: \n30: ### Priority Levels\n31: \n32: | Level | Meaning | Enforcement |\n33: |-------|---------|-------------|\n34: | P0 | HARD BLOCKER | Must fix before commit |\n35: | P1 | Required | Must fix or get explicit approval |\n36: | P2 | Recommended | Can defer with justification |\n37: \n38: ---\n39: \n40: ## 2. P0 - HARD BLOCKERS\n41: \n42: These items MUST be fixed before any commit.\n43: \n44: ### Box Header\n45: \n46: ```markdown\n47: [ ] File has the module header naming the module\n48: ```\n49: \n50: **Required format** (the `MODULE:` divider header from the style guide, the same\n51: form TypeScript files use, with 67 box-drawing characters per divider):\n52: ```javascript\n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // MODULE: [Module Name]\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: ```\n57: \n58: A shebang may precede the header. A new file never takes the older 78-character\n59: `╔═╗` box or the two-row `COMPONENT:`/`PURPOSE:` header. Files that already carry\n60: either one keep it until they are next rewritten.\n61: \n62: ### 'use strict'\n63: \n64: ```markdown\n65: [ ] 'use strict' directive present at top of file\n66: ```\n67: \n68: **Required** for `.js/.cjs` files (immediately after header):\n69: ```javascript\n70: 'use strict';\n71: ```\n72: \n73: **Exception**: `.mjs` files and OpenCode plugin ESM paths are strict by module\n74: semantics; the verifier intentionally skips `.mjs` strict-mode enforcement.\n75: \n76: ### No Commented-Out Code\n77: \n78: ```markdown\n79: [ ] No commented-out code blocks\n80: ```\n81: \n82: **Remove**:\n83: ```javascript\n84: // function old_implementation() {\n85: //     return \"deprecated\";\n86: // }\n87: ```\n88: \n89: ### WHY Comments\n90: \n91: ```markdown\n92: [ ] Purposeful WHY comments only (max 3 comments per 10 LOC)\n93:     - Comments explain WHY, not WHAT\n94:     - No narrative comments describing obvious mechanics\n95: ```\n96: \n97: **Required for**:\n98: - Algorithm choices\n99: - Performance optimizations\n100: - Business rule implementations\n101: - Workarounds for known issues\n102: \n103: ### Numbered ALL-CAPS Sections\n104: \n105: ```markdown\n106: [ ] Numbered ALL-CAPS section headers are preserved (`## N. SECTION NAME`)\n107: ```\n108: \n109: ### Plugin Runtime Output\n110: \n111: ```markdown\n112: [ ] OpenCode plugin never writes to stdout/stderr (would overlay the TUI chat input)\n113: ```\n114: Surface stale/guard notices via `experimental.chat.system.transform` (bounded\n115: `output.system` push), a fail-open log file, or a plugin tool — `stderr` only\n116: behind a default-off debug env flag. Reference: `system-dist-freshness-guard.js`.\n117: See `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6.\n118: \n119: ---\n120: \n121: ## 3. P1 - REQUIRED\n122: \n123: These must be addressed or receive approval to defer.\n124: \n125: ### Module Exports by Path\n126: \n127: ```markdown\n128: [ ] Module export style matches the file path and package boundary\n129: ```\n130: \n131: **Correct for non-plugin `.js/.cjs` utilities**:\n132: ```javascript\n133: module.exports = { functionName, CONSTANT };\n134: module.exports = functionName;\n135: ```\n136: \n137: **Correct for OpenCode plugin loader paths**:\n138: ```javascript\n139: export default async function ExamplePlugin(ctx, options = {}) {\n140:   return {};\n141: }\n142: ```\n143: \n144: **Plugin paths**: Files in `.opencode/plugins/` MUST use ESM (`export default`)\n145: — see `../../references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md` §6 SKILLED PLUGIN EXEMPTION TIER.\n146: \n147: **Wrong for non-plugin CommonJS utilities**:\n148: ```javascript\n149: export { function_name };\n150: export default function_name;\n151: ```\n152: \n153: ### Guard Clauses\n154: \n155: ```markdown\n156: [ ] Functions use early returns for invalid states\n157: ```\n158: \n159: **Correct**:\n160: ```javascript\n161: function processData(data) {\n162:     if (!data) return null;\n163:     if (!data.requiredField) return null;\n164: \n165:     // Main logic here\n166:     return result;\n167: }\n168: ```\n169: \n170: **Wrong**:\n171: ```javascript\n172: function processData(data) {\n173:     if (data) {\n174:         if (data.requiredField) {\n175:             // Deeply nested logic\n176:         }\n177:     }\n178: }\n179: ```\n180: \n181: ### Bracketed Logging\n182: \n183: ```markdown\n184: [ ] Console output uses bracketed format\n185: ```\n186: \n187: **Correct**:\n188: ```javascript\n189: console.log('[COMPONENT] Processing file:', filename);\n190: console.error('[COMPONENT] Error:', error.message);\n191: ```\n192: \n193: **Wrong**:\n194: ```javascript\n195: console.log('Processing file: ' + filename);\n196: console.log(filename);  // No context\n197: ```\n198: \n199: ### Function Naming\n200: \n201: ```markdown\n202: [ ] All functions use camelCase naming\n203: ```\n204: \n205: **Correct**:\n206: ```javascript\n207: function validateInput(data) { }\n208: function processFile(path) { }\n209: const handleError = (err) => { };\n210: ```\n211: \n212: **Wrong**:\n213: ```javascript\n214: function validate_input(data) { }    // snake_case\n215: function ProcessFile(path) { }       // PascalCase\n216: ```\n217: \n218: ### Constants\n219: \n220: ```markdown\n221: [ ] Module-level constants use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE\n222: ```\n223: \n224: **Correct**:\n225: ```javascript\n226: const MAX_RETRIES = 3;\n227: const DEFAULT_TIMEOUT = 5000;\n228: const VALID_TYPES = ['a', 'b', 'c'];\n229: ```\n230: \n231: ### Error Handling\n232: \n233: ```markdown\n234: [ ] Try/catch blocks handle errors appropriately\n235: [ ] Error messages include context\n236: ```\n237: \n238: **Correct**:\n239: ```javascript\n240: try {\n241:     const data = JSON.parse(content);\n242: } catch (error) {\n243:     console.error('[PARSER] Failed to parse JSON:', error.message);\n244:     throw new Error(`Invalid JSON in ${filename}: ${error.message}`);\n245: }\n246: ```\n247: \n248: ### KISS / DRY / SOLID Checks\n249: \n250: ```markdown\n251: [ ] KISS: avoid unnecessary abstraction or layering\n252: [ ] DRY: repeated rules/constants extracted to shared source\n253: [ ] SOLID: SRP/OCP/LSP/ISP/DIP violations checked and documented\n254: ```\n255: \n256: ---\n257: \n258: ## 4. P2 - RECOMMENDED\n259: \n260: These improve quality but can be deferred.\n261: \n262: ### JSDoc Comments\n263: \n264: ```markdown\n265: [ ] Public functions have JSDoc documentation\n266: ```\n267: \n268: **Example**:\n269: ```javascript\n270: /**\n271:  * Validates input data against schema.\n272:  * @param {Object} data - The data to validate\n273:  * @param {Object} schema - Validation schema\n274:  * @returns {Object} Validation result with isValid and errors\n275:  */\n276: function validateInput(data, schema) { }\n277: ```\n278: \n279: ### Consistent String Quotes\n280: \n281: ```markdown\n282: [ ] Strings use single quotes consistently\n283: ```\n284: \n285: **Preferred**:\n286: ```javascript\n287: const message = 'Hello world';\n288: const config = { key: 'value' };\n289: ```\n290: \n291: ### Async/Await Pattern\n292: \n293: ```markdown\n294: [ ] Async operations use async/await (not callbacks where possible)\n295: ```\n296: \n297: **Preferred**:\n298: ```javascript\n299: async function fetchData() {\n300:     const response = await fetch(url);\n301:     const data = await response.json();\n302:     return data;\n303: }\n304: ```\n305: \n306: ### Destructuring\n307: \n308: ```markdown\n309: [ ] Object destructuring used where it improves clarity\n310: ```\n311: \n312: **Good**:\n313: ```javascript\n314: const { name, value } = config;\n315: function process({ data, options = {} }) { }\n316: ```\n317: \n318: ---\n319: \n320: ## 5. CHECKLIST TEMPLATE\n321: \n322: For formal findings-first review output, use `sk-code`'s code-review mode as the baseline and treat this file as JavaScript surface evidence.\n323: \n324: ```markdown\n325: ## JavaScript Standards Surface Evidence\n326: \n327: - [ ] JavaScript standards validated in `javascript-checklist.md`\n328: - [ ] Universal standards validated in `universal-checklist.md`\n329: - [ ] Findings severity/order produced with `sk-code/sk-code-review/references/quick-reference.md`\n330: - [ ] Baseline security, quality, and test checks sourced from `sk-code`'s code-review mode\n331: - [ ] Surface-specific deviations documented with file:line evidence\n332: ```\n333: \n334: ---\n335: \n336: ## 6. VALIDATION COMMANDS\n337: \n338: ```bash\n339: # Syntax check\n340: node --check file.js\n341: \n342: # ESLint (if configured)\n343: npx eslint file.js\n344: \n345: # Check for common issues in non-plugin CommonJS utilities\n346: grep -n \"function [a-z]*_[a-z]\" file.js  # Find snake_case functions\n347: grep -n \"^export \" file.js              # Review: allowed only for ESM/plugin paths\n348: ```\n349: \n350: ---\n351: \n352: ## 7. RELATED RESOURCES\n353: \n354: ### Checklists\n355: - [universal-checklist.md](./universal-checklist.md) - Language-agnostic checks\n356: \n357: ### Style Guide\n358: - [JavaScript Style Guide](../../references/javascript/style-guide.md)\n359: - [JavaScript Quality Standards](../../references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md)"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794527417825,"end":1794527418153}}}}
